WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:04.060
BOAS: Excuse me.

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:05.200
If I could have everybody's attention really
quickly.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.790
Sorry to interrupt but we're going to get
started in just a moment, but I just wanted

00:00:09.790 --> 00:00:11.230
to point out this is a little bit different
from other Term Member events.

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:12.790
This is actually going to be on the record,
so just keep that in mind.

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:13.790
We're streaming it live and it will be posted
afterward.

00:00:13.790 --> 00:00:14.790
So just wanted to let you all know.

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:15.790
Please turn off your cellphones too, if you
don't mind.

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:16.790
Thanks.

00:00:16.790 --> 00:00:17.790
Are we ready?

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:18.790
Great.

00:00:18.790 --> 00:00:19.790
We're going to get started.

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:20.790
So for all of you in the back, just take a
seat wherever.

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:21.790
We're meeting on global entrepreneurship with
Elmira-is it Bayrasli?

00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:22.790
BAYRASLI: Yes.

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:23.790
BOAS: And Christopher Schroeder.

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:24.900
In the spirit of our topic, we're live-streaming
this.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:29.210
So I also want to welcome CFR members and
others joining us from around the world.

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:32.770
And just another reminder that we're on the
record tonight.

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:37.071
You should have bios of Elmira and Christopher
in your packs and online, but briefly, both

00:00:37.071 --> 00:00:41.720
have written extensively about entrepreneurship
and innovation around the world, Christopher

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:45.830
in the Middle East and Elmira seemingly everywhere
else.

00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:50.650
Christopher's book is "Startup Rising: The
Entrepreneurial Revolution Remaking the Middle

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:56.390
East" and Elmira's is "From the Other Side
of the World: Extraordinary Entrepreneurs,

00:00:56.390 --> 00:00:57.390
Unlikely Places."

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:00.990
In addition, Christopher and Elmira and entrepreneurs
themselves.

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:06.400
Christopher has run several e-commerce businesses
and invested in dozens more and Elmira is

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:10.719
the cofounder of Foreign Policy Interrupted,
which aims to increase the number of women

00:01:10.719 --> 00:01:13.630
commenting on foreign affairs in the press.

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:15.960
Christopher and Elmira, thank you for being
here tonight.

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:19.899
I should start by noting that I have a new
strategy for finding great books, which is

00:01:19.899 --> 00:01:22.039
to moderate more CFR panels.

00:01:22.039 --> 00:01:23.039
(Laughter.)

00:01:23.039 --> 00:01:24.560
So let's begin by talking about the books.

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:29.240
I'd love to know what inspired each of you
to write them.

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:37.469
BAYRASLI: I think for me it was-as the daughter
of immigrants from Turkey to the United States,

00:01:37.469 --> 00:01:42.549
I think there is always this perception about
Americans and "the other."

00:01:42.549 --> 00:01:46.939
And for some reason, we tend to think everything
in America is great, and for the most part

00:01:46.939 --> 00:01:47.939
it is.

00:01:47.939 --> 00:01:53.499
I think that that-you know, I think that there's
a reason that America is an economic leader,

00:01:53.499 --> 00:01:59.399
I think a leader around the world, but I also
think that there are-there's no lack of great

00:01:59.399 --> 00:02:03.819
ideas, whether it's in Africa, Asia, Latin
America, or the Middle East.

00:02:03.819 --> 00:02:08.600
And when we talk about entrepreneurship here
in the United States, we automatically think

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:14.290
of people like Steve Jobs or Bill Gates or
Michael Dell, and we think about the big technology

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:15.739
companies out of Silicon Valley.

00:02:15.739 --> 00:02:20.930
And the minute you say "entrepreneurship"
in the context of Africa, Asia, Latin America,

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:22.980
the Middle East, it becomes something different.

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:24.620
It becomes something very micro.

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:29.760
It becomes something that-you know, women
doing handcrafts.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:35.540
And what I was seeing is that while there's
certainly that, there's certainly women doing

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:42.340
handcrafts here in the-in Brooklyn, in fact,
but there are men and women around the world,

00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:48.519
whether they're in Nigeria, South Africa,
Brazil, Jordan, in Malaysia, that are leading

00:02:48.519 --> 00:02:52.959
really globally competitive companies in all
fields, not just technology.

00:02:52.959 --> 00:02:59.160
Whether it's in energy or biotech or in health
care, they're really doing very innovative

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:00.160
things.

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:04.910
And what I really wanted to do is write the
stories of these men and women-how they're

00:03:04.910 --> 00:03:10.510
doing it, why are they doing it now-to really
shatter the paradigm about what entrepreneurship

00:03:10.510 --> 00:03:13.870
is and that it can only be done here in the
United States.

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:19.180
SCHROEDER: So my journey is not as personal
but is actually quite similar in some respects,

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:24.040
and it really has been a journey of-of fighting
narrative bias is probably the way to say

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:25.040
about you.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:28.830
Because I have traveled all over the world,
the companies I've built outsource technology

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:32.859
to almost any growth market that you could
name, but the idea that the kinds of things

00:03:32.859 --> 00:03:36.159
that Elmira just described as happening around
the world could be happening in those days

00:03:36.159 --> 00:03:39.349
in Mubarak's Egypt was completely off of my
narrative.

00:03:39.349 --> 00:03:42.219
My narrative was the CNN narrative and there
was only one thing to say about the Middle

00:03:42.219 --> 00:03:44.530
East, and that was sort of the end of it overall.

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:47.750
And again, I've been there as a tourist, I
have many friends there, but I still couldn't

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:48.750
believe it.

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:53.689
And I got to know a couple of remarkable men-actually
a bunch of people but two men in particular

00:03:53.689 --> 00:03:58.511
who started talking about how universal access
to technology was changing society in the

00:03:58.511 --> 00:03:59.530
Middle East around 2010.

00:03:59.530 --> 00:04:04.200
It was around the time, of course, where that
young man burned himself to death in Tunisia.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:07.400
And obviously something else was happening
at the time but they said: You don't understand.

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:10.780
I mean, all you guys think about is al-Qaida
and September 11th.

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:14.989
The new generation has access to this technology
and they're doing amazing things to it.

00:04:14.989 --> 00:04:19.000
And they put together what was the first real
large gathering of startups in the Arab world

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:22.590
in the fall of 2010, which they called Celebration
of Entrepreneurship.

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:24.890
And they dragged me there pretty much kicking
and screaming.

00:04:24.890 --> 00:04:28.550
There's a guy named Fadi Ghandour, who is
really kind of a legend over there, who himself

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:29.550
was an entrepreneur.

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:34.539
He built Aramex, which is the largest logistics
company in the Middle East and Africa now,

00:04:34.539 --> 00:04:38.970
and a man named Arif Naqvi, who has built
Abraaj Capital, who some of you know is one

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:41.550
of the largest-the largest private equity
fund.

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:43.110
And they dragged me over there.

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:47.360
And in many respects, I view my life as before
that weekend and after that weekend, because

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:50.870
nobody was talking about Israel, no one gave
a damn about Obama's Cairo speech.

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:54.590
They were all doing what we all take for granted
in New York and Silicon Valley and just building

00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:55.590
amazing things.

00:04:55.590 --> 00:05:00.389
It was 3,000 young people from around the
region, 3,000 other young people who tried

00:05:00.389 --> 00:05:02.780
to get in and couldn't, were on a waiting
list.

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:07.240
And it was just unbelievably riveting and
made me feel like an idiot because I thought

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:08.800
I should know better about this.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:13.430
So I actually extended my trip and I went
to Egypt and Jordan and I saw the whole thing

00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:15.060
over and over again.

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:18.910
And on the plane ride back-I used to run the
online stuff for the Washington Post and Newsweek,

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:22.510
and on the plane ride back I wrote an op-ed
about what I saw.

00:05:22.510 --> 00:05:26.330
And Hyatt wasn't too into publishing it but
at the minute said, well, this is interesting

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:27.470
enough; maybe I'll do that.

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:30.500
And you guys know, all you have to do is write
an op-ed somewhere and everyone thinks you're

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:32.090
an expert on the subject.

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:36.139
So like the next day, two very moving things
happened to me about my reflection.

00:05:36.139 --> 00:05:39.150
One is I got literally hundreds of emails
from the region.

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:42.790
I don't know how the people found me, but
they found me just to say thank you for talking

00:05:42.790 --> 00:05:46.440
about something other than the bombs and explosions
and terrorism but the Middle East.

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:50.060
And the other thing I did was I got a phone
call from a remarkable man in Mrs. Clinton's

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:52.810
office saying: You're obviously an expert
in this field.

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:54.699
We're having startup competition in January.

00:05:54.699 --> 00:05:56.850
Would you mind to come judge it?

00:05:56.850 --> 00:05:59.900
And, you know, I said, you know, I'd like
to understand this more.

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:00.900
I have plenty of vacation time.

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:01.900
And I did.

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:05.389
And I was there-if you were paying attention
to the calendar-in January 2011.

00:06:05.389 --> 00:06:10.879
And one week after I was there-well, I was
in Syria when Ben Ali fell, which was a mind-blowing

00:06:10.879 --> 00:06:15.470
experience in and of itself, but then in Egypt
every one of the hundreds of young entrepreneurs

00:06:15.470 --> 00:06:19.360
I met, all as impressive as the last round,
of course were in Tahrir Square.

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:23.449
And then at that point I knew that I would
start figuring out a way to get this message

00:06:23.449 --> 00:06:27.100
out, because I think a lot of Americans didn't
understand it and I felt that a lot of young

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:29.190
people were doing this in the region.

00:06:29.190 --> 00:06:30.919
Their parents didn't understand it.

00:06:30.919 --> 00:06:32.669
So I thought it was an important story to
tell.

00:06:32.669 --> 00:06:37.190
BOAS: So, Christopher, you write that if you
have one hope for your book it's that it will

00:06:37.190 --> 00:06:40.780
inspire people to rethink our conceptions
about the Middle East and engage.

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:43.490
And you say there's a Middle East out there
that you've never heard of and it's coming

00:06:43.490 --> 00:06:44.580
on strong.

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:47.620
To your point from just now, can you tell
us why?

00:06:47.620 --> 00:06:50.669
SCHROEDER: Well, let me give it to you in
a poignant example and something to think

00:06:50.669 --> 00:06:51.669
about.

00:06:51.669 --> 00:06:52.669
And as a businessman, I think about it all
the time.

00:06:52.669 --> 00:06:55.980
And you, in your ambitions, I hope if you
remember me for nothing else you will remember

00:06:55.980 --> 00:06:58.830
me for this, and that is the power of narrative
bias.

00:06:58.830 --> 00:07:00.790
Now, for all-you know, stories are important.

00:07:00.790 --> 00:07:01.870
We have to tell stories.

00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:03.210
It helps us do business plans.

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:06.310
It helps us explain to our family why we do
what we do.

00:07:06.310 --> 00:07:10.550
But you can get caught very, very simply and
make profound mistakes in so doing it.

00:07:10.550 --> 00:07:15.560
So to put it in perspective, in December I
was in Cairo, Egypt at a large startup gathering

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:19.990
of over 5,000 young people who were just doing,
again, the same kind of things that I talked

00:07:19.990 --> 00:07:20.990
about before.

00:07:20.990 --> 00:07:23.620
Elmira was there with me in a similar one
in Istanbul.

00:07:23.620 --> 00:07:28.199
As interestingly, 24 hours before that one
in Cairo a different 5,000 young people were

00:07:28.199 --> 00:07:30.180
gathered in Beirut, Lebanon.

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:35.150
Now, I can assure you that if 10,000 young
people were gathered in the Arab world to

00:07:35.150 --> 00:07:38.470
talk about the Muslim Brotherhood, we'd all
in this room know about it.

00:07:38.470 --> 00:07:42.139
None of us, I submit, knew what I just told
you.

00:07:42.139 --> 00:07:46.100
And so this is happening at speed because
it's not just about the Middle East.

00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:50.439
It is about-I can't tell you what Syria is
going to look like in a year or what, you

00:07:50.439 --> 00:07:53.310
know, Egypt will look like in three, but I
can tell you with 100 percent certainty, by

00:07:53.310 --> 00:07:57.189
the end of this decade two-thirds of the human
race and two-thirds of the Middle East are

00:07:57.189 --> 00:07:58.620
going to be walking around with smart devices.

00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:01.520
It's already bigger than that in the Gulf.

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:02.639
And these are supercomputers.

00:08:02.639 --> 00:08:04.300
These aren't just fancy phones.

00:08:04.300 --> 00:08:07.470
These are folks who are walking around with
supercomputers in their pockets.

00:08:07.470 --> 00:08:09.870
They see the way the rest of the world acts.

00:08:09.870 --> 00:08:11.170
They want pieces of the other things.

00:08:11.170 --> 00:08:12.360
They collaborate with each other.

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:15.470
They feel empowered because someone who looks
like them has done something successfully

00:08:15.470 --> 00:08:17.220
so that means I can do it as well.

00:08:17.220 --> 00:08:19.320
And that's not going back.

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:23.530
And so, to me, the narrative bias is deeply
embedded in our society.

00:08:23.530 --> 00:08:24.940
It's embedded by the media.

00:08:24.940 --> 00:08:28.159
It's embedded by our own instincts of where
we spend our time.

00:08:28.159 --> 00:08:31.560
And it's something that I think not only we
can check, but for us who keep going around

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:35.110
in Washington thinking we can take the same
hammer over and over again and think every

00:08:35.110 --> 00:08:39.800
problem in the Middle East is ISIS you can
almost predict perfectly how that will play

00:08:39.800 --> 00:08:43.720
out over time, whereas for the first time
we have something here that I keep pushing

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:47.529
my friends to think about, which is how should
we be thinking about problem solving for a

00:08:47.529 --> 00:08:52.550
new generation, which is empowered, two-thirds
of which are walking around with smart devices?

00:08:52.550 --> 00:08:53.829
What does that change?

00:08:53.829 --> 00:08:56.950
You can argue it changes nothing, if in fact
you think about it.

00:08:56.950 --> 00:08:59.080
And part of the problem is nobody thinks about
it.

00:08:59.080 --> 00:09:02.649
And so that's what I think is important for
all of us to consider.

00:09:02.649 --> 00:09:08.200
BOAS: So, speaking of narrative, Elmira, you
may think that I didn't make it past page

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:12.590
2 by asking this, but you start your book
with an anecdote about a trip to visit your

00:09:12.590 --> 00:09:15.260
family in Turkey when you were 8.

00:09:15.260 --> 00:09:19.089
You were disappointed at what you had here
but not there, including ketchup for your

00:09:19.089 --> 00:09:20.089
fries.

00:09:20.089 --> 00:09:21.300
BAYRASLI: There wasn't ketchup.

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:23.070
It was very disappointing.

00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:24.070
(Laughter.)

00:09:24.070 --> 00:09:29.290
BOAS: So when you asked why, your grandmother
told you, this isn't America and it's so much

00:09:29.290 --> 00:09:31.170
trouble to start a business in Turkey.

00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:36.740
Then you wrote, "In America, anything and
everything was possible, but not in Turkey."

00:09:36.740 --> 00:09:42.130
Fast-forward a few decades and the next 250
pages disprove that, with stories of entrepreneurship

00:09:42.130 --> 00:09:46.070
and possibility in Turkey and the half-dozen
other non-obvious places.

00:09:46.070 --> 00:09:49.750
To what extent do you think things are now
possible in those unlikely places?

00:09:49.750 --> 00:09:52.440
And can you tell us if you got your ketchup?

00:09:52.440 --> 00:09:53.440
(Laughter.)

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:57.040
BAYRASLI: I didn't, and it was actually the
first time that I actually really understood

00:09:57.040 --> 00:10:04.410
why my parents had immigrated to the United
States, you know, this concept that my grandmother

00:10:04.410 --> 00:10:06.170
had made fries and I wanted ketchup.

00:10:06.170 --> 00:10:11.750
And, you know, I had been prepared for, you
know, the dirt roads and there was no television

00:10:11.750 --> 00:10:15.620
and the electricity was going off, but when
she told me there was no ketchup I, like-I

00:10:15.620 --> 00:10:20.930
started crying and I was just like, this is
the most terrible place in the world.

00:10:20.930 --> 00:10:25.470
And the comment about, you know, it's not-because
I said, why can't someone just start a ketchup

00:10:25.470 --> 00:10:26.470
factory?

00:10:26.470 --> 00:10:27.480
I don't understand.

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:28.839
And she said, well, you just can't do that
here.

00:10:28.839 --> 00:10:33.770
And it was the first time that I actually-it
dawned on me that that was one of the reasons

00:10:33.770 --> 00:10:38.769
that my parents made the difficult decision
to immigrate to the United States, because

00:10:38.769 --> 00:10:42.690
there was opportunity in the United States,
that you couldn't just do that-you couldn't

00:10:42.690 --> 00:10:44.420
do that in Turkey.

00:10:44.420 --> 00:10:49.500
I think what Chris was touching on now, and
why it's possible now, because now in Turkey,

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:56.860
I mean, there's so-the entrepreneurial ecosystem
in Istanbul, Ankara, and throughout the country,

00:10:56.860 --> 00:11:03.730
it's really booming and it's really amazing
to see, you know, so many years-I won't exactly

00:11:03.730 --> 00:11:08.500
say how many-after-(chuckles)-after that incident.

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:09.829
And I think a couple of things.

00:11:09.829 --> 00:11:16.089
I think the technology that has spread so
far so you have Internet penetration and mobile

00:11:16.089 --> 00:11:22.710
phones, and I think that the technology has
enabled individuals to solve their own problems.

00:11:22.710 --> 00:11:26.450
And they're not dependent on whether it's
the government to actually go out there and

00:11:26.450 --> 00:11:29.410
fix their roads or to communicate with one
another.

00:11:29.410 --> 00:11:30.950
They have access to information.

00:11:30.950 --> 00:11:34.820
They can figure out the financial component
of it.

00:11:34.820 --> 00:11:36.440
They can move money.

00:11:36.440 --> 00:11:39.170
The technology has enabled them to do that.

00:11:39.170 --> 00:11:44.310
I also think globalization has played a big
role in just transforming the economic landscape

00:11:44.310 --> 00:11:45.310
throughout the world.

00:11:45.310 --> 00:11:50.779
And I think that, you know, outsourcing has
transferred knowledge but it's also raised

00:11:50.779 --> 00:11:51.850
incomes.

00:11:51.850 --> 00:11:56.930
And where you actually see entrepreneurship
happening, it doesn't happen with the poorest

00:11:56.930 --> 00:12:00.180
of the poor and it doesn't happen with the
richest of the rich.

00:12:00.180 --> 00:12:02.000
It happens in the middle class.

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:07.200
It happens with people who see basic problems,
they see gaps in the market, and they have

00:12:07.200 --> 00:12:12.050
the disposable income, they have the opportunity
to actually go in there and solve those problems.

00:12:12.050 --> 00:12:19.220
So as you've seen fortunes rise in countries
like Brazil, India, Russia, and China-where,

00:12:19.220 --> 00:12:23.829
coincidentally, hand in hand, there have been
a lot of entrepreneurs-I think you're starting

00:12:23.829 --> 00:12:32.480
to see the barriers that previously have prevented
individuals in those places from taking an

00:12:32.480 --> 00:12:38.240
idea, replicating it, and then scaling it
up to really break down those barriers.

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:44.220
And my book is very much structured in that
way, where I take a look at the obstacles

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:50.070
that have prevented individuals outside the
United States from replicating the Silicon

00:12:50.070 --> 00:12:55.050
Valley story-so things like corruption and
poor infrastructure and the lack of creative

00:12:55.050 --> 00:12:57.930
spaces and the lack of competition.

00:12:57.930 --> 00:13:02.550
And globalization and technology has just
turned that on its head and you have pioneering

00:13:02.550 --> 00:13:08.699
individuals who understand the challenges
in their countries.

00:13:08.699 --> 00:13:14.180
And the men and women in the book really are-they
really understand problem solving.

00:13:14.180 --> 00:13:20.089
And I actually really had an epiphany in Nigeria
about what problems we have.

00:13:20.089 --> 00:13:24.360
And as somebody who's worked in the government,
you know, I had automatically assumed that

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:29.301
the obstacle in Nigeria which held Nigeria
back was poor infrastructure, because you

00:13:29.301 --> 00:13:34.389
go to Nigeria and in Lagos itself, you know,
the electricity goes out, the roads are bad,

00:13:34.389 --> 00:13:40.080
and the traffic is just terrible and there's
really-public transportation really is nonexistent.

00:13:40.080 --> 00:13:44.710
You think public transportation-you think
poor infrastructure is the challenge.

00:13:44.710 --> 00:13:49.759
And when I dug a lot deeper and talked to
the entrepreneur that I profile in my book,

00:13:49.759 --> 00:13:56.850
Tayo Oviosu, who runs a startup called Paga,
what I actually understood is that these obstacles

00:13:56.850 --> 00:14:03.180
such as poor infrastructure are a symptom
and not necessarily the root problem, because

00:14:03.180 --> 00:14:07.959
the problem in Nigeria and why you have poor
infrastructure is because they have a lack

00:14:07.959 --> 00:14:12.560
of a financial framework and the inability
to move money has never allowed money to be

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:19.410
an asset and to be leveraged and put back
into the society where it can then be put

00:14:19.410 --> 00:14:25.060
into bridges and roads and the electricity
system and all of these things.

00:14:25.060 --> 00:14:31.500
And so what I'm finding is that, you know,
there is no lack of ideas or talent around

00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:32.670
the world.

00:14:32.670 --> 00:14:35.940
There is lack of opportunity, but I think
as Chris pointed out, in the Middle East I

00:14:35.940 --> 00:14:40.820
think people are using the technology, you
know, tapping into it and they're eager to

00:14:40.820 --> 00:14:42.160
solve their own problems.

00:14:42.160 --> 00:14:44.949
BOAS: So you talk about technology leveling
the playing field.

00:14:44.949 --> 00:14:50.680
What's it going to take for folks for whom
the playing field has been leveled to leapfrog

00:14:50.680 --> 00:14:55.040
ahead of the more traditional places like
Silicon Valley?

00:14:55.040 --> 00:15:00.680
BAYRASLI: You know, one of the reasons I wanted
to go to Nigeria and particularly look at

00:15:00.680 --> 00:15:05.860
the mobile phone space is because you constantly
hear this word "leap-frogging" and that how

00:15:05.860 --> 00:15:11.420
the mobile phone is going to help Africa leapfrog
and join, you know, Silicon Valley and everybody

00:15:11.420 --> 00:15:13.980
else in the 21st century.

00:15:13.980 --> 00:15:19.980
I think technology, what's helping, whether
it's in Nigeria or in Kenya where M-Pesa got

00:15:19.980 --> 00:15:25.790
started, the original mobile money payment
platform, is that it's helping them catch

00:15:25.790 --> 00:15:26.790
up.

00:15:26.790 --> 00:15:31.839
And I think it's really-it's a little bit
disingenuous to actually talk about leap-frogging.

00:15:31.839 --> 00:15:36.010
I think it's enabling those individuals to
catch up but doing it in a way where they're

00:15:36.010 --> 00:15:38.960
tapping into their own innovations.

00:15:38.960 --> 00:15:45.930
Because there's a challenge of moving money
in a lot of places in Africa, what the mobile

00:15:45.930 --> 00:15:51.460
phone has done is it's enabled-whether it's
in Kenya or in Nigeria-for those individuals

00:15:51.460 --> 00:15:57.399
to actually use a medium and a mechanism to
move the money around and innovate on that

00:15:57.399 --> 00:16:00.530
platform, because it's a challenge that they
have.

00:16:00.530 --> 00:16:02.550
It's not a challenge that we have here in
the United States.

00:16:02.550 --> 00:16:06.870
We have credit cards, we have bank accounts;
we have mechanisms where we already have a

00:16:06.870 --> 00:16:08.220
financial framework.

00:16:08.220 --> 00:16:13.110
I think what's interesting that technology
is enabling individuals to do, whether it's

00:16:13.110 --> 00:16:18.910
in Nigeria or Brazil or in other parts of
the world, is that it's enabling them to solve

00:16:18.910 --> 00:16:21.279
challenges that we don't actually face here.

00:16:21.279 --> 00:16:26.250
And what I'm excited about is that we're seeing
just the first generation.

00:16:26.250 --> 00:16:28.779
You know, this is just like the-I just think
it's the beginning.

00:16:28.779 --> 00:16:29.779
SCHROEDER: Absolutely.

00:16:29.779 --> 00:16:36.269
BAYRASLI: And I just think it's so exciting
to see how rapidly even the entrepreneurs

00:16:36.269 --> 00:16:44.860
that I saw five years ago are now really thinking
on par with those in Silicon Valley about

00:16:44.860 --> 00:16:50.100
how do you actually now innovate and take
these ideas to scale, and ideas that we certainly

00:16:50.100 --> 00:16:52.420
would not think of here in the United States.

00:16:52.420 --> 00:16:57.589
And they're iterating on them and they're
experimenting with them in a way where I actually

00:16:57.589 --> 00:17:00.779
think in the next generation, and certainly
in the third generation, we're going to see,

00:17:00.779 --> 00:17:04.020
really, the next big thing come out, outside
of Silicon Valley.

00:17:04.020 --> 00:17:06.160
SCHROEDER: Can I add to that?

00:17:06.160 --> 00:17:07.160
BOAS: Please.

00:17:07.160 --> 00:17:11.390
SCHROEDER: So a lot of times I get asked,
particularly in Silicon Valley, what is going

00:17:11.390 --> 00:17:12.540
to be the next Silicon Valley?

00:17:12.540 --> 00:17:14.080
And I actually think it's completely the wrong
question.

00:17:14.080 --> 00:17:16.720
I don't think it's actually relevant in the
21st century.

00:17:16.720 --> 00:17:21.360
Let me caveat that by saying Silicon Valley
is an absolutely extraordinary place, with

00:17:21.360 --> 00:17:23.760
all the hubris that sometimes gets associated
with it.

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:27.580
I mean, it's up there with, you know, C�rdoba
a thousand years ago.

00:17:27.580 --> 00:17:29.170
It's up there with Renaissance Florence.

00:17:29.170 --> 00:17:33.440
I mean, it is an incredible network effect
of talent where unbelievable people want to

00:17:33.440 --> 00:17:36.160
be around unbelievable people and they get
the spiral of it.

00:17:36.160 --> 00:17:40.170
Secondly, as a corollary to that, one has
to be cautious about thinking about innovation

00:17:40.170 --> 00:17:44.350
in these other markets and what that means,
which I'll come back to in a second.

00:17:44.350 --> 00:17:47.380
But, you know, there's a reason why the Facebook,
the Twitter, the Snapchat, the LinkedIn-of

00:17:47.380 --> 00:17:52.090
almost market where the governments haven't
blocked them is Facebook, Twitter, and Snapchat

00:17:52.090 --> 00:17:53.090
and LinkedIn.

00:17:53.090 --> 00:17:57.390
I mean, there is an incredible skill and ability
that when these companies scale in software,

00:17:57.390 --> 00:17:58.390
they tend to dominate powerfully.

00:17:58.390 --> 00:18:01.010
By the way, that happens in America too.

00:18:01.010 --> 00:18:06.080
I mean, Salesforce-I see a lot of enterprise
software companies rising and Salesforce still-they

00:18:06.080 --> 00:18:07.080
have to deal with them.

00:18:07.080 --> 00:18:11.110
I mean, so these juggernauts, when they get
big because of technology, become hard to

00:18:11.110 --> 00:18:14.400
go up against in America, let alone in these
other markets overall.

00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:18.640
There's a wonderful, by the way-on Adreessen
Horowitz's a16z site they did a wonderful

00:18:18.640 --> 00:18:23.011
podcast on Africa, where a couple of the entrepreneurs
pointed out in it-they're like-you know, one

00:18:23.011 --> 00:18:26.150
of them tried to do the WeChat or the Whatsapp
of Africa.

00:18:26.150 --> 00:18:27.150
They thought this was a great idea.

00:18:27.150 --> 00:18:30.830
And of course they got flattened because everyone
was using Whatsapp.

00:18:30.830 --> 00:18:32.300
And they asked, what's your conclusion of
that?

00:18:32.300 --> 00:18:35.110
And they said, never go up directly against
the Americans.

00:18:35.110 --> 00:18:39.810
And I think that's right, but now let's talk
about what innovation means, because we in

00:18:39.810 --> 00:18:43.080
the West tend to think of innovation as a
bright, shiny new thing, and thank god for

00:18:43.080 --> 00:18:44.080
Elon Musk.

00:18:44.080 --> 00:18:45.080
I'm all in.

00:18:45.080 --> 00:18:46.080
I invest in those companies.

00:18:46.080 --> 00:18:47.080
I love them.

00:18:47.080 --> 00:18:48.080
It's awesome.

00:18:48.080 --> 00:18:52.090
But tell me that someone who could not move
a hundred dollars 20 miles without doing it

00:18:52.090 --> 00:18:55.940
physically four years ago who can now do it
with a multiplier effect in terms of their

00:18:55.940 --> 00:19:00.000
personal safety, in terms of the life they
live, in terms of expanding even a small crafts

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:02.490
business to reach another market-tell me that's
not innovation.

00:19:02.490 --> 00:19:06.260
And I'll tell you about one of my favorite
companies I've seen lately, because I've been

00:19:06.260 --> 00:19:08.860
traveling not just in the Middle East but
I've been expanding it as well.

00:19:08.860 --> 00:19:12.540
But there's an amazing company called Twiga
in Kenya.

00:19:12.540 --> 00:19:17.890
Forty-two-thousand fruit vendors are in Nairobi
alone, 42,000.

00:19:17.890 --> 00:19:21.890
Every one of them gets up at 4:00 in the morning
to walk two-and-a-half hours to be able to

00:19:21.890 --> 00:19:25.780
get their inventory of bananas or whatever
fruit they're going to sell, OK?

00:19:25.780 --> 00:19:27.600
There is no inventory management system.

00:19:27.600 --> 00:19:31.310
They have amazing instincts about what they
need, but if they stock out by noon there's

00:19:31.310 --> 00:19:33.230
no getting more supplies anywhere else.

00:19:33.230 --> 00:19:35.430
It's just not a great, ideal circumstance.

00:19:35.430 --> 00:19:40.340
So, says the couple of founders in the region,
what if we could make the distribution system

00:19:40.340 --> 00:19:45.440
much more effective, use technology to bring
containers closer to the city and, where we

00:19:45.440 --> 00:19:51.840
could, with Tuk Tuks at almost the same cost,
deliver the fruit to these vendors?

00:19:51.840 --> 00:19:55.410
What if, at the same time, you either got
for them one of the very cheap-or gave away

00:19:55.410 --> 00:20:00.370
because now smart devices are $25 a pop and
are very, very good-what if you gave them

00:20:00.370 --> 00:20:03.720
that, gave them a little bit of a data plan
by which they could order every day what they

00:20:03.720 --> 00:20:06.534
want and they could do it, then they could
order more than once a day, which means they

00:20:06.534 --> 00:20:08.150
could grow their business.

00:20:08.150 --> 00:20:12.170
What if, with all the data that you get on
who's paying and what they're doing and how

00:20:12.170 --> 00:20:16.160
their business is growing, you could then
determine how to give them instant micro loans

00:20:16.160 --> 00:20:19.250
right then, because you have more data about
them and their ability to pay than anyone

00:20:19.250 --> 00:20:20.420
else on earth?

00:20:20.420 --> 00:20:21.460
That's a big three.

00:20:21.460 --> 00:20:24.310
I mean, think about the potential ramifications
of that.

00:20:24.310 --> 00:20:25.810
Now, tell me that's not innovation?

00:20:25.810 --> 00:20:28.770
But do I think Amazon is going to do that
anytime soon?

00:20:28.770 --> 00:20:30.170
I really don't.

00:20:30.170 --> 00:20:32.810
And I'm wrong all the time, but I don't.

00:20:32.810 --> 00:20:36.590
And so I think that it's very interesting,
as we think about innovation in these markets,

00:20:36.590 --> 00:20:41.080
on the market's terms, the understanding of
your backyard, the understanding of the dynamics

00:20:41.080 --> 00:20:44.550
I've just described, the understanding of
local government regulation, the understanding

00:20:44.550 --> 00:20:48.860
of cultural sensitivities that big juggernauts
on the platforms don't necessarily understand

00:20:48.860 --> 00:20:49.990
very well.

00:20:49.990 --> 00:20:53.040
These things can matter and can scale very
large.

00:20:53.040 --> 00:20:57.040
And the ramifications, I think, in the economy
across the boards are deeply profound and

00:20:57.040 --> 00:20:59.200
are absolutely, in my view, innovative.

00:20:59.200 --> 00:21:05.460
BOAS: Can you talk about something that, as
you've done this work, that had been surprising

00:21:05.460 --> 00:21:06.540
to you?

00:21:06.540 --> 00:21:13.760
BAYRASLI: I think for me what's been surprising
is what Chris touched on about the 5,000 people

00:21:13.760 --> 00:21:22.360
that we saw in December in Cairo just north
of Tahrir Square, you know, 5,000 Arabs coming

00:21:22.360 --> 00:21:27.360
together to talk about entrepreneurship and
ideas and innovation and, you know, to pitch

00:21:27.360 --> 00:21:32.400
investors and to do-and to do these things.

00:21:32.400 --> 00:21:37.460
You know, it's not so much-I have to say I'm
always-I'm not surprised but I'm impressed

00:21:37.460 --> 00:21:42.280
with the men and women that I do meet around
the world and the ideas that they have-and,

00:21:42.280 --> 00:21:48.420
you know, just from, like, water filtration
systems to different ways of, you know, selling

00:21:48.420 --> 00:21:55.030
retail for different clientele, different
alternative energy companies and leveraging

00:21:55.030 --> 00:22:01.100
the use of technology for alternative energy,
you know, delivering services that people

00:22:01.100 --> 00:22:05.860
have never had like insurance, and the different
mechanisms that they're using.

00:22:05.860 --> 00:22:10.470
And I'm always impressed by that, but I think
the thing that really surprises me is why

00:22:10.470 --> 00:22:14.600
we're not getting it here in the United States,
why we're not getting-what is happening around

00:22:14.600 --> 00:22:20.130
the world and why we have not adjusted, whether
it is our narrative and our world point of

00:22:20.130 --> 00:22:22.750
view, but also our foreign policy for that
matter.

00:22:22.750 --> 00:22:24.110
SCHROEDER: Why do you think we're missing
it?

00:22:24.110 --> 00:22:25.470
Tell them why you think we're missing it.

00:22:25.470 --> 00:22:30.850
BAYRASLI: I think because we still have-you
know, we have the institutional mechanisms,

00:22:30.850 --> 00:22:33.860
whether it's the media or the way that Washington
is structured.

00:22:33.860 --> 00:22:38.650
And, you know, in the last chapter of my book
I look at China, and the obstacle in China

00:22:38.650 --> 00:22:40.380
is the status quo.

00:22:40.380 --> 00:22:44.680
And I wrote that very much with the United
States in mind, because when you look at companies

00:22:44.680 --> 00:22:50.110
like Uber and Airbnb, they're coming up against
the establishment.

00:22:50.110 --> 00:22:56.170
And that is the biggest barrier for entrepreneurs
here in the United States is, you know, we

00:22:56.170 --> 00:23:00.950
don't have a lot of challenges here in this
country, and so where are you going to actually

00:23:00.950 --> 00:23:02.130
iterate and innovate?

00:23:02.130 --> 00:23:06.470
And, you know, yes, thank-you know, thank
goodness for the Elon Musks of the world,

00:23:06.470 --> 00:23:11.590
but, you know, at the end of the day, though,
there's also a limited amount of, you know,

00:23:11.590 --> 00:23:16.990
thinking about the future and AI and virtual
reality.

00:23:16.990 --> 00:23:20.110
And the next innovations and where they're
going to be happening is going to be coming

00:23:20.110 --> 00:23:23.800
outside of the United States, and we're not
thinking about that.

00:23:23.800 --> 00:23:28.440
We're not paying attention to what is happening
out there.

00:23:28.440 --> 00:23:32.840
You know, and the one criticism I do have
of Silicon Valley is that they're not-to a

00:23:32.840 --> 00:23:34.430
certain extent they're not paying attention
to it either.

00:23:34.430 --> 00:23:36.290
You know, it's not just Washington.

00:23:36.290 --> 00:23:39.910
Silicon Valley is not paying attention to
it either.

00:23:39.910 --> 00:23:45.300
And the men and women that I have met, whether
it is in Nigeria or South Africa or Brazil

00:23:45.300 --> 00:23:51.610
and Mexico-I was just in Malaysia two weeks
ago and, you know, looking at-hearing entrepreneurs

00:23:51.610 --> 00:23:54.190
there, and it's just-it really, truly is stunning.

00:23:54.190 --> 00:23:58.730
And I think that we need to be paying attention
much more to what's happening around the world

00:23:58.730 --> 00:24:02.310
because there are serious problems out there
and there are people working on them to solve

00:24:02.310 --> 00:24:03.700
them.

00:24:03.700 --> 00:24:09.080
SCHROEDER: I think in some respects, at least
on the Silicon Valley side of it, that-what's

00:24:09.080 --> 00:24:11.990
the e-commerce juggernaut that just went public
from China a year ago?

00:24:11.990 --> 00:24:12.990
BAYRASLI: Alibaba.

00:24:12.990 --> 00:24:13.990
SCHROEDER: Alibaba.

00:24:13.990 --> 00:24:15.790
So I think Alibaba was actually an interesting
wakeup call.

00:24:15.790 --> 00:24:18.900
And it's been very interesting with friends
of mine in Silicon Valley who have looked

00:24:18.900 --> 00:24:21.930
at it in this lens, which is not just that
it's a big juggernaut, because if you-you

00:24:21.930 --> 00:24:25.830
know, if you watch the press about Alibaba
they say, of course another inside job from

00:24:25.830 --> 00:24:30.890
Washington-from Beijing; China just made billions
of dollars, because that's what happens there.

00:24:30.890 --> 00:24:33.440
They pick their winners and winners win, and
everything else.

00:24:33.440 --> 00:24:37.420
But the story, when you stop to think about
it, is here's a company that came from nowhere,

00:24:37.420 --> 00:24:42.200
that raised, you know, a lot of money and
then now has billions of dollars in revenue,

00:24:42.200 --> 00:24:45.310
virtually none of which has anything to do
with the West at all.

00:24:45.310 --> 00:24:48.480
And stop and think about what I just said,
because for all intents and purposes, over

00:24:48.480 --> 00:24:52.790
the last 60 years, to make it in the global
economy let alone to make it in technology,

00:24:52.790 --> 00:24:54.020
meant to make it in the West.

00:24:54.020 --> 00:24:56.150
It actually really meant to make it in America.

00:24:56.150 --> 00:24:59.650
So if you even think about hardware in the
'70s and '80s-so you think about the folks

00:24:59.650 --> 00:25:02.830
in consumer electronics, in Sony and that
kind of stuff-when they talked about becoming

00:25:02.830 --> 00:25:06.780
global companies, they became global because
they made it in America.

00:25:06.780 --> 00:25:09.930
Software companies still, to this day, if
they are successful elsewhere, have to be

00:25:09.930 --> 00:25:11.380
able to get into our market.

00:25:11.380 --> 00:25:16.160
Here is a tech-enabled juggernaut of multibillions
of dollars which effectively did it without

00:25:16.160 --> 00:25:17.590
the West at all.

00:25:17.590 --> 00:25:20.130
That's the kind of thing that makes people
think, maybe we have to look at our narrative

00:25:20.130 --> 00:25:24.980
a little bit differently, and yet the gravitational
pull still is to be so focused here.

00:25:24.980 --> 00:25:28.150
As you know, I've got a lot of friends in
the media business, and I really kicked their

00:25:28.150 --> 00:25:29.150
ass on this subject.

00:25:29.150 --> 00:25:30.640
It drives me crazy.

00:25:30.640 --> 00:25:34.790
The Cairo event I mentioned to you earlier,
there were 40 journalists there and maybe

00:25:34.790 --> 00:25:35.790
three from the West.

00:25:35.790 --> 00:25:40.230
And one of them took off to go to the Turkish
border to cover the disaster there, which

00:25:40.230 --> 00:25:42.020
is fine.

00:25:42.020 --> 00:25:46.060
Nothing that I'm saying should say that ISIS
is irrelevant or-this is a very tough neighborhood.

00:25:46.060 --> 00:25:47.980
I'm not saying that narrative is wrong.

00:25:47.980 --> 00:25:52.490
There's a lot of tough stuff that we cannot
predict for sure in many parts of the world,

00:25:52.490 --> 00:25:55.610
but I'm saying here's something else that
should be given at least equal time.

00:25:55.610 --> 00:25:58.470
And the kinds of things that you hear from
the TV journalist friends of mine are like,

00:25:58.470 --> 00:26:02.350
do you know how hard it is to get foreign
anything in the news today?

00:26:02.350 --> 00:26:05.780
So if we have 30 seconds of a news hole to
talk about something in the Middle East and

00:26:05.780 --> 00:26:08.720
everyone else is talking about ISIS, we're
not going to talk about ISIS?

00:26:08.720 --> 00:26:12.110
I'm like, you know, but what does that mean?

00:26:12.110 --> 00:26:15.340
Well, that's not really-what you're talking
about is not really news, Chris.

00:26:15.340 --> 00:26:16.340
It's not news.

00:26:16.340 --> 00:26:17.340
It's like these are happy stories.

00:26:17.340 --> 00:26:18.780
We do happy features from time to time.

00:26:18.780 --> 00:26:20.710
I'm like, well, you don't understand.

00:26:20.710 --> 00:26:24.900
Go back to the point that I made about the
10,000 young people and the Muslim Brotherhood.

00:26:24.900 --> 00:26:28.040
That's a news event because you're worried
that that might have some ramifications on

00:26:28.040 --> 00:26:29.530
the future of society.

00:26:29.530 --> 00:26:33.320
The 10,000 young people that we get to see
all the time are absolutely changing their

00:26:33.320 --> 00:26:34.320
society.

00:26:34.320 --> 00:26:37.790
It may be a different trajectory but it's
a complete different shift.

00:26:37.790 --> 00:26:41.930
And so if I leave you with nothing here-because
you guys are amazing at what you do, you're

00:26:41.930 --> 00:26:46.380
amazing who you know-is to be able to push
people to say, we need to be thinking about

00:26:46.380 --> 00:26:51.140
that in a different way, I think is important
because I'm telling you, man, this-in Washington

00:26:51.140 --> 00:26:54.290
I will sit down at events like-like, I like
being a Term Member better.

00:26:54.290 --> 00:26:55.430
Can I just tell you for the record?

00:26:55.430 --> 00:26:56.430
(Laughter.)

00:26:56.430 --> 00:26:57.840
It was a lot of fun here being with you guys
and everything else.

00:26:57.840 --> 00:27:01.220
Now, when I go to the old people stuff it's
like we're still talking about the Cold War

00:27:01.220 --> 00:27:02.220
half the time, right?

00:27:02.220 --> 00:27:03.220
(Laughter.)

00:27:03.220 --> 00:27:05.840
And so, like, when we talk about the stuff
that we're talking about now it's like we

00:27:05.840 --> 00:27:08.120
need-cybersecurity is a big issue.

00:27:08.120 --> 00:27:09.120
Yes, it is.

00:27:09.120 --> 00:27:10.980
We need a START treaty for cybersecurity.

00:27:10.980 --> 00:27:14.500
It's like, you clearly don't understand technology
at all-(laughter)-like, at all.

00:27:14.500 --> 00:27:15.610
Like, this is a network thing.

00:27:15.610 --> 00:27:16.610
It's, like, totally different.

00:27:16.610 --> 00:27:20.730
And so, you know, I'm just unbelievably enthusiastic
to be with you guys because you're able to

00:27:20.730 --> 00:27:23.300
understand the foundations of what I'm seeing.

00:27:23.300 --> 00:27:26.600
Many of you have lived it or seen it, and
you have a huge opportunity to constantly

00:27:26.600 --> 00:27:31.980
be an apostle in conveying how important this
is to the world and to this country.

00:27:31.980 --> 00:27:36.740
BOAS: So that seems like a perfect opportunity
to make the former Western journalist stop

00:27:36.740 --> 00:27:39.290
asking questions and open the floor to you
all.

00:27:39.290 --> 00:27:43.260
I'm supposed to remind you that this meeting
is on the record.

00:27:43.260 --> 00:27:44.260
SCHROEDER: Do we still do that?

00:27:44.260 --> 00:27:45.260
Even as a Term Member we tilt our cards?

00:27:45.260 --> 00:27:46.260
(Laughter.)

00:27:46.260 --> 00:27:47.260
BOAS: You can put yours up too if you'd like.

00:27:47.260 --> 00:27:48.260
SCHROEDER: Yeah, I feel very good about that.

00:27:48.260 --> 00:27:49.260
That's good.

00:27:49.260 --> 00:27:50.260
BOAS: Yeah.

00:27:50.260 --> 00:27:51.260
So as a reminder, this is on the record.

00:27:51.260 --> 00:27:57.720
Also, you probably all know how to use the
technology, but to speak, push the button,

00:27:57.720 --> 00:28:01.210
and then push it again to turn it off.

00:28:01.210 --> 00:28:05.450
And make sure to state your name and affiliation,
and to keep your question to a question.

00:28:05.450 --> 00:28:06.460
Anish?

00:28:06.460 --> 00:28:09.480
Q: Thank you.

00:28:09.480 --> 00:28:12.120
Anish Melwani, LVMH.

00:28:12.120 --> 00:28:18.180
I'm curious as to whether you believe-how
much of this is really a businesspeople here

00:28:18.180 --> 00:28:20.950
don't see this happening versus a media problem?

00:28:20.950 --> 00:28:25.950
My personal experience is that the media problem
you describe is absolutely accurate, but I

00:28:25.950 --> 00:28:33.720
hear a lot of talk about how to access markets-Middle
East, Africa, Southeast Asia-and how companies

00:28:33.720 --> 00:28:37.230
here are trying to use local entrepreneurs
to do it because they know that they can't

00:28:37.230 --> 00:28:38.230
do it themselves.

00:28:38.230 --> 00:28:42.740
BAYRASLI: I mean, I think that-I think businesspeople
are interested.

00:28:42.740 --> 00:28:47.990
And actually, interestingly, I went to more
than two dozen countries to do the research

00:28:47.990 --> 00:28:52.991
in my book, and I came down to the seven primarily
because the publisher said, you know, focus

00:28:52.991 --> 00:28:59.360
on the markets that Wall Street is interested
in, you know, Nigeria, Mexico, India, Russia,

00:28:59.360 --> 00:29:00.360
China.

00:29:00.360 --> 00:29:04.660
Those are the markets that the money people
here in New York are interested in.

00:29:04.660 --> 00:29:08.930
And so certainly there is interest there and
people are following those markets, but it's

00:29:08.930 --> 00:29:15.180
very much from that still the traditional
banking, traditional finance, private equity.

00:29:15.180 --> 00:29:19.020
They're seeing there's a lot of development
and there's certainly construction.

00:29:19.020 --> 00:29:23.480
Construction is a huge boom in pretty much
every emerging market, and so there's certainly

00:29:23.480 --> 00:29:25.510
a lot of wealth to be tapped into there.

00:29:25.510 --> 00:29:30.670
But when we're talking about traditional-whether
it's angel investing or venture capital investing,

00:29:30.670 --> 00:29:38.010
you still see very-those investors from the
West are still hesitant to go into those markets.

00:29:38.010 --> 00:29:45.400
And so I think there's a handful of the Silicon
Valley investors-the Sand Hill Road investors-that

00:29:45.400 --> 00:29:47.740
have gone out.

00:29:47.740 --> 00:29:53.380
I profiled an entrepreneur in my book from
Pakistan who managed to get Tim Draper and

00:29:53.380 --> 00:29:58.310
Tim Draper's venture capital fund to invest
in his firm in Pakistan despite all the bombings

00:29:58.310 --> 00:29:59.310
that were going on.

00:29:59.310 --> 00:30:02.530
And it was the year that Benazir Bhutto was
assassinated.

00:30:02.530 --> 00:30:05.070
And he still manages-he managed to convince
it.

00:30:05.070 --> 00:30:10.640
And I go into an extensive look at, well,
what is venture capital if it's not-if it's

00:30:10.640 --> 00:30:11.640
not actually risk?

00:30:11.640 --> 00:30:15.580
SCHROEDER: Well, the beauty of this world
that we're talking about has a two-edged sword

00:30:15.580 --> 00:30:19.300
to it, which is it is happening everywhere,
which means as a hardnosed businesswoman you

00:30:19.300 --> 00:30:22.440
have to-you have an opportunity to make decisions
which you want to make.

00:30:22.440 --> 00:30:26.130
And so you think about the basics like rule
of law and those kinds of things, relative

00:30:26.130 --> 00:30:27.660
stability, and you certainly look at market
size.

00:30:27.660 --> 00:30:32.770
I mean, China and India has been such an early
lead in this for all the obvious reasons because

00:30:32.770 --> 00:30:33.770
it's a market.

00:30:33.770 --> 00:30:37.700
But what never ceases to amaze me is how many
Americans don't appreciate that when you talk

00:30:37.700 --> 00:30:40.030
about, say-let's use the Arab world as an
example of this.

00:30:40.030 --> 00:30:44.470
There's 350 million people who speak Arabic
in a very sizable market whose per-capita

00:30:44.470 --> 00:30:47.750
income is larger than India's, right?

00:30:47.750 --> 00:30:51.030
And all of a sudden-if you haven't been to
Dubai in the last year, go to Dubai, man.

00:30:51.030 --> 00:30:55.010
I mean, it is a different story, what's happening
there, and the government is serious as a

00:30:55.010 --> 00:30:59.730
heart attack, with ministers, a third or more
of whom are women, who are-many of the rest

00:30:59.730 --> 00:31:01.480
are under the age of 35.

00:31:01.480 --> 00:31:05.970
And they are on a mission to try to not only
bring that city, and then the UAE writ large,

00:31:05.970 --> 00:31:07.620
but are trying to bring things with them.

00:31:07.620 --> 00:31:10.980
So a lot of it has to do with getting caught
up and seeing the speed of this and see where

00:31:10.980 --> 00:31:14.030
things are going, I think in very powerful
ways.

00:31:14.030 --> 00:31:17.880
But it does mean that even businesspeople,
to be able to ask these questions, have to

00:31:17.880 --> 00:31:21.059
say, when I think Middle East I think ISIS
and Raqqa.

00:31:21.059 --> 00:31:25.170
And I keep telling them, you should be thinking
about Souq.com in Dubai also.

00:31:25.170 --> 00:31:26.370
Both are true.

00:31:26.370 --> 00:31:31.610
Souq.com was just valued by Naspers and by
Tiger and some other great investors at over

00:31:31.610 --> 00:31:32.610
a billion-and-a-half.

00:31:32.610 --> 00:31:35.120
It's the largest e-commerce platform in the
Arab world right now.

00:31:35.120 --> 00:31:39.790
When numbers like that start to come and people
know that the whole world has challenges and

00:31:39.790 --> 00:31:43.320
you have to navigate risk and the challenges
that you do, I think people then start to

00:31:43.320 --> 00:31:45.590
have a different lens and decide to start
going.

00:31:45.590 --> 00:31:48.990
The smart ones know they can only do it in
a sense of co-authorship.

00:31:48.990 --> 00:31:52.540
I mean, the big enterprises understand that
if you don't understand the ground and work

00:31:52.540 --> 00:31:54.960
with the ground on the ground's terms, you're
dead in the water.

00:31:54.960 --> 00:31:59.410
I think early on, a fair number of the Silicon
Valley people who rushed to China-because

00:31:59.410 --> 00:32:02.770
you have to be there-I mean, they kind of
arrived like, I'm Silicon Valley, man; aren't

00:32:02.770 --> 00:32:03.770
you lucky to have me here?

00:32:03.770 --> 00:32:04.800
Man, they got their lunch eaten.

00:32:04.800 --> 00:32:05.800
BAYRASLI: Right.

00:32:05.800 --> 00:32:06.800
SCHROEDER: Right?

00:32:06.800 --> 00:32:11.440
And so there is a phase that comes with this
too in the sense of reciprocity where I think,

00:32:11.440 --> 00:32:17.290
as success breeds success, we won't be having
this conversation in five years, certainly

00:32:17.290 --> 00:32:18.290
not from the business community.

00:32:18.290 --> 00:32:20.570
The media I don't know.

00:32:20.570 --> 00:32:22.030
BOAS: Bobbi?

00:32:22.030 --> 00:32:26.900
Q: I am Bobbi Thomason from the Wharton School.

00:32:26.900 --> 00:32:29.110
So thank you both for your comments.

00:32:29.110 --> 00:32:35.040
I'm huge fans of your work for not only, I
think, shining a light on amazing entrepreneurship

00:32:35.040 --> 00:32:41.210
that's happening in emerging markets but for
the relationship that you both are creating

00:32:41.210 --> 00:32:48.460
between the U.S./the West/Silicon Valley and
these markets to disrupt what I think we can

00:32:48.460 --> 00:32:54.850
call the hierarchy, where we associate the
sort of serious concept of entrepreneurship,

00:32:54.850 --> 00:32:59.240
the Steve Jobs figure, in fact only happening
in the U.S.

00:32:59.240 --> 00:33:07.780
I'm curious for your thoughts on other individuals
or organizations in the U.S. and Silicon Valley

00:33:07.780 --> 00:33:15.310
that are also engaging with entrepreneurs
in these markets in a sort of peer "we have

00:33:15.310 --> 00:33:17.260
something to learn" and disrupting that hierarchy.

00:33:17.260 --> 00:33:19.100
What are they doing?

00:33:19.100 --> 00:33:26.910
You know, what else could actors in the U.S.
be doing to engage in, I think, a new way

00:33:26.910 --> 00:33:27.910
that both of you are?

00:33:27.910 --> 00:33:30.720
BAYRASLI: Do you want to-
SCHROEDER: Yeah, go ahead.

00:33:30.720 --> 00:33:35.000
BAYRASLI: I mean, there's certainly-there's
certainly organizations both here, Silicon

00:33:35.000 --> 00:33:36.430
Valley, and in Washington.

00:33:36.430 --> 00:33:41.010
You know, Chris and I, just last month we
were in Medellin, Colombia with the Global

00:33:41.010 --> 00:33:45.580
Entrepreneurship Network, that started, you
know, 10 years ago doing Global Entrepreneurship

00:33:45.580 --> 00:33:46.580
Week.

00:33:46.580 --> 00:33:50.841
And, you know, it's grown into-it's 80 countries
participated in that.

00:33:50.841 --> 00:33:52.730
Was that right?

00:33:52.730 --> 00:33:57.420
People from around the world coming together,
you know, to have this discussion about what

00:33:57.420 --> 00:34:01.820
is happening in terms of entrepreneurship
around the world.

00:34:01.820 --> 00:34:04.550
And there's certainly no lack of, I think,
organizations.

00:34:04.550 --> 00:34:05.550
Endeavor is one.

00:34:05.550 --> 00:34:10.790
I used to work at Endeavor, which is a nonprofit
that's headquartered here in New York City

00:34:10.790 --> 00:34:15.040
that supports what they call high-impact entrepreneurs
around the world.

00:34:15.040 --> 00:34:18.990
I think they just opened up an office in Japan.

00:34:18.990 --> 00:34:20.660
Good for them.

00:34:20.660 --> 00:34:25.950
But I also think that-I think that you're
starting to-it's one of these things where

00:34:25.950 --> 00:34:32.940
you have, on both ends-where you have the
individuals within these countries doing grassroots,

00:34:32.940 --> 00:34:33.980
you know, innovative things.

00:34:33.980 --> 00:34:40.010
But I think what's so interesting to see is
the connections of the individuals who had

00:34:40.010 --> 00:34:46.260
experience in Silicon Valley going back to
seize on the opportunities in their own countries.

00:34:46.260 --> 00:34:52.530
And so Tayo Oviosu, who I write about in my
book, actually was working at Cisco in Silicon

00:34:52.530 --> 00:34:58.570
Valley and decided that he was going to go
back to Nigeria, where he was born and he

00:34:58.570 --> 00:35:04.670
grew up, not because at the time Nigeria had
become the economic powerhouse it had but

00:35:04.670 --> 00:35:10.010
he thought, well, if it's happening in India
and China, it's got to happen in Nigeria sometime

00:35:10.010 --> 00:35:11.010
soon.

00:35:11.010 --> 00:35:14.830
And so you have these individuals-my entrepreneur
Bulent Celebi, who I profile in my Turkey

00:35:14.830 --> 00:35:16.110
chapter, you know, the same story.

00:35:16.110 --> 00:35:17.590
You know, he was 49 years old.

00:35:17.590 --> 00:35:23.971
He was the CEO of a tech company in Silicon
Valley and he decided, I'm going to go back

00:35:23.971 --> 00:35:26.980
and I'm going to start a wireless router company
in Istanbul.

00:35:26.980 --> 00:35:28.160
And this was in 2004.

00:35:28.160 --> 00:35:33.480
This was not when Turkey had, you know, become
the economic powerhouse that it had.

00:35:33.480 --> 00:35:37.290
And so I think where you're really starting
to see the disruption is not necessarily from

00:35:37.290 --> 00:35:40.300
organizations but it's from these pioneering
individuals.

00:35:40.300 --> 00:35:46.820
And I think it really goes hand in hand with
what entrepreneurship is, because ultimately

00:35:46.820 --> 00:35:49.620
it is about disruption and it's about changing
mindsets.

00:35:49.620 --> 00:35:55.060
So, you know, I do take to task-and I'm glad
that you brought up the whole-you know, the

00:35:55.060 --> 00:35:56.770
concept of innovation.

00:35:56.770 --> 00:36:01.990
You know, we associate entrepreneurship with
innovation, and that can really be a very

00:36:01.990 --> 00:36:05.710
tangled-a tangled relationship.

00:36:05.710 --> 00:36:09.090
Entrepreneurship doesn't necessarily have
to be a shiny new product.

00:36:09.090 --> 00:36:12.390
It's actually just going out there and doing
something in a new way.

00:36:12.390 --> 00:36:18.220
And I actually define entrepreneurship as
remaking the socioeconomic landscape.

00:36:18.220 --> 00:36:20.400
And that is what has been missing around the
world.

00:36:20.400 --> 00:36:25.730
And now you've having individuals who are
taking that risk and going back to the countries

00:36:25.730 --> 00:36:30.100
where, you know, their parents hailed from,
where they originally were from, and they're

00:36:30.100 --> 00:36:35.660
doing it because they see that the world has
changed and there is an opportunity to do

00:36:35.660 --> 00:36:36.660
it.

00:36:36.660 --> 00:36:41.670
And they're pioneering it in a way where a
lot of people are getting on board with it.

00:36:41.670 --> 00:36:43.420
SCHROEDER: How many of you have seen "Narcos,"
by the way?

00:36:43.420 --> 00:36:44.660
Any of you watch "Narcos"?

00:36:44.660 --> 00:36:45.850
It's awesome, isn't it?

00:36:45.850 --> 00:36:47.350
Hey, I urge it completely.

00:36:47.350 --> 00:36:49.000
But stop and think what she said almost parenthetically,
right?

00:36:49.000 --> 00:36:52.990
We're in a gathering of thousands of young
people, many of whom were from Medellin, which

00:36:52.990 --> 00:36:56.290
was, 20 years ago, a complete war zone of
historic proportions.

00:36:56.290 --> 00:37:01.750
Hundreds of thousands of people died in that
conflict, which people forget about now today.

00:37:01.750 --> 00:37:06.170
And here it is one of the fastest-growing
economies in the-in Latin America.

00:37:06.170 --> 00:37:07.540
It is a jewel of the region.

00:37:07.540 --> 00:37:08.660
It's absolutely beautiful country.

00:37:08.660 --> 00:37:11.540
Medellin was an incredibly vibrant city and
there were these great entrepreneurs doing

00:37:11.540 --> 00:37:12.540
things.

00:37:12.540 --> 00:37:15.690
The reason why that's important is because
when people go around and say, like, you know,

00:37:15.690 --> 00:37:19.940
these places that are disasters, they need
America, they need to be engaged, and maybe

00:37:19.940 --> 00:37:23.110
they'll never come back, and don't bother
with Iraq or the refugee crisis because it

00:37:23.110 --> 00:37:26.430
could never happen, well, you know, look at
Colombia.

00:37:26.430 --> 00:37:33.070
And I am involved with an organization called
Gaza Sky Geeks, which is-it's a mind-blow

00:37:33.070 --> 00:37:37.170
incubator of hundreds of entrepreneurs in
Gaza right now solving local problems, trying

00:37:37.170 --> 00:37:41.140
to solve problems beyond Gaza, but unfortunately,
because of the way technology is connected,

00:37:41.140 --> 00:37:42.770
it's a little bit hard for them to do.

00:37:42.770 --> 00:37:45.060
But these entrepreneurs are rock stars, man.

00:37:45.060 --> 00:37:48.650
They're just awesome and tenacious and fearless
and what have you.

00:37:48.650 --> 00:37:52.950
To your question, Gaza Sky Geeks got funded
in part by Mercy Corps.

00:37:52.950 --> 00:37:55.960
So there are a lot of different groups who
are thinking creatively about how to be able

00:37:55.960 --> 00:38:00.340
to fund and co-author, but there is no substitute
for people who actually get on a plane and

00:38:00.340 --> 00:38:02.710
go to the places and engage on their terms.

00:38:02.710 --> 00:38:07.920
Dave McClure, God bless him, of 500 startups,
shows up, man, I mean to great personal sacrifice.

00:38:07.920 --> 00:38:11.910
This guy is in a different country all the
time, showing up, building relationships with

00:38:11.910 --> 00:38:14.530
people on the ground and co-authoring, not
top-down.

00:38:14.530 --> 00:38:22.480
But I think the most important point is I
think the diaspora population can be a huge

00:38:22.480 --> 00:38:25.790
bridge the way that Elmira talked about so
beautifully before.

00:38:25.790 --> 00:38:29.280
I do think people are not only going back
there, but people who remain here, that's

00:38:29.280 --> 00:38:30.410
an amazing connection.

00:38:30.410 --> 00:38:32.800
We've seen it happen beautifully, particularly
with India.

00:38:32.800 --> 00:38:34.830
We've seen it happen beautifully with Israel.

00:38:34.830 --> 00:38:39.410
All that's important but it's happening anyway.

00:38:39.410 --> 00:38:43.600
No one is waiting around for America to engage
other than they would love to be able to do

00:38:43.600 --> 00:38:47.440
business development stuff, co-author in learning,
find ways to work together and everything

00:38:47.440 --> 00:38:48.440
else.

00:38:48.440 --> 00:38:54.230
Eight years ago-five years ago-eight years
ago these sort of classic stories was the

00:38:54.230 --> 00:38:57.270
kind of story that she talked about, where
if you were a Chinese American who went back

00:38:57.270 --> 00:38:59.990
to China, that was a perfect bridge to be
able to invest into.

00:38:59.990 --> 00:39:04.430
It is stunning to me now how many venture
capital operations and entrepreneurs themselves

00:39:04.430 --> 00:39:05.910
are Chinese first.

00:39:05.910 --> 00:39:07.650
They're not, anymore, as much of that story.

00:39:07.650 --> 00:39:11.070
And you're just going to see more and more
of that everywhere, which just means, one,

00:39:11.070 --> 00:39:14.080
the opportunity is enormous but it is a co-authored
opportunity.

00:39:14.080 --> 00:39:16.480
It is one where everyone brings something
to the table.

00:39:16.480 --> 00:39:18.540
No one is sitting around waiting for us.

00:39:18.540 --> 00:39:21.170
But on the other hand-I'll give you an example.

00:39:21.170 --> 00:39:22.660
They love us for this.

00:39:22.660 --> 00:39:24.310
That's the other side of the coin.

00:39:24.310 --> 00:39:28.180
So there's one of the largest shared workspaces
I write a little bit about in the book.

00:39:28.180 --> 00:39:31.660
One of the largest shared workspaces in the
Arab world is in the old American University

00:39:31.660 --> 00:39:33.830
of Cairo in downtown, right by Tahrir Square.

00:39:33.830 --> 00:39:36.490
You could throw a baseball at Tahrir Square
from where it's located.

00:39:36.490 --> 00:39:39.240
And it's just a really beautiful renovated
space they've done.

00:39:39.240 --> 00:39:41.540
Thousands of young people are there on a regular
basis.

00:39:41.540 --> 00:39:44.630
They give office space to businesses and that
kind of thing.

00:39:44.630 --> 00:39:45.650
It's very vibrant.

00:39:45.650 --> 00:39:48.110
It's where this big gathering we went to was
hosted.

00:39:48.110 --> 00:39:50.740
Anyway, about a year ago there was a smaller
gathering.

00:39:50.740 --> 00:39:53.710
There was, like, maybe a thousand people,
you know, milling about and everything else.

00:39:53.710 --> 00:39:57.810
And as I arrived there, I came in, and in
the background I could see these huge photographs

00:39:57.810 --> 00:39:58.810
of men.

00:39:58.810 --> 00:40:00.820
And I thought, oh, Sisi co-opted this one.

00:40:00.820 --> 00:40:03.950
I mean, I figured these are big political
figures and the time has come.

00:40:03.950 --> 00:40:08.770
And I get closer and closer, and it's Jack
Dorsey and Mark Zuckerberg and Elon Musk,

00:40:08.770 --> 00:40:10.500
and that's what they put up there.

00:40:10.500 --> 00:40:15.020
So there's a lot of strength in an understanding
of the power of co-authorship, but nor is

00:40:15.020 --> 00:40:18.660
there anyone waiting around hoping that we'll
figure this out.

00:40:18.660 --> 00:40:20.320
BOAS: Daniel?

00:40:20.320 --> 00:40:21.980
Q: Hi.

00:40:21.980 --> 00:40:25.070
Dan O'Keefe from Technology Crossover Ventures.

00:40:25.070 --> 00:40:30.190
We are an admittedly Silicon Valley-based,
later-stage technology investment firm, but

00:40:30.190 --> 00:40:32.690
we invest on a global basis.

00:40:32.690 --> 00:40:36.930
And as I was thinking about the conversation-we
talked quite a bit about how a mobile device

00:40:36.930 --> 00:40:41.570
and the fabric of information enables entrepreneurship,
but it does occur to me that there's a flip

00:40:41.570 --> 00:40:45.790
side to the coin, which is what-how you enable
and attract institutional capital, right,

00:40:45.790 --> 00:40:49.640
at scale, which obviously the U.S. has done
quite effectively and certain regions have

00:40:49.640 --> 00:40:51.310
done quite effectively.

00:40:51.310 --> 00:40:57.270
And some of what I would argue will attract
institutional capital at scale are institutions

00:40:57.270 --> 00:41:01.500
controlled by state actors, right, so things
like established contract law, bankruptcy

00:41:01.500 --> 00:41:06.070
protection or some form of downside protection
that enables risk taking.

00:41:06.070 --> 00:41:09.520
And I'm just curious if you've seen examples
where this groundswell of entrepreneurship

00:41:09.520 --> 00:41:15.680
activity-you know, this kind of bottoms-up
activity, has created any institutions to

00:41:15.680 --> 00:41:18.450
adapt to a more entrepreneurial level of activity.

00:41:18.450 --> 00:41:22.190
We've seen that in the political side of things,
right, where institutions are challenged by

00:41:22.190 --> 00:41:24.220
the information flow that a mobile device
enables.

00:41:24.220 --> 00:41:26.640
I'm just curious if you've seen that happen
in entrepreneurship.

00:41:26.640 --> 00:41:29.080
BAYRASLI: Why don't you take that one?

00:41:29.080 --> 00:41:32.570
SCHROEDER: So a couple things.

00:41:32.570 --> 00:41:35.230
It is a bottom-up-there's a wonderful quote
in my book.

00:41:35.230 --> 00:41:39.270
For those of you who speak Arabic, or particularly
Lebanese Arabic, there's this wonderful word

00:41:39.270 --> 00:41:40.700
called "wasta."

00:41:40.700 --> 00:41:42.610
And wasta is effectively, like, who do you
know?

00:41:42.610 --> 00:41:44.370
That's effectively what it means.

00:41:44.370 --> 00:41:46.810
And it's a pejorative statement.

00:41:46.810 --> 00:41:49.240
It's like, if you don't know somebody, you
can't get anything done, and everything else

00:41:49.240 --> 00:41:50.240
like that.

00:41:50.240 --> 00:41:54.250
Now, coming from Washington I know wasta very
well, but it is a different-I mean, because

00:41:54.250 --> 00:41:55.250
it's a cancer, right?

00:41:55.250 --> 00:41:58.200
I mean, if you're in a big organization and
you're a young, ambitious person, and somebody's

00:41:58.200 --> 00:42:02.820
on top of you and you'll never get over that
person because wasta has protected him-it

00:42:02.820 --> 00:42:04.370
actually goes deep in the system and everything
else.

00:42:04.370 --> 00:42:08.230
Anyways, to your question, one of my favorite
quotes-two-I have two favorite quotes in the

00:42:08.230 --> 00:42:12.100
book, and one of them comes from Fadi Ghandour
who said, you know, there is no wasta on the

00:42:12.100 --> 00:42:13.100
Internet.

00:42:13.100 --> 00:42:14.510
And what he means by that is twofold.

00:42:14.510 --> 00:42:18.980
One is you're able to move so quickly and
do things so quickly that by the time you're

00:42:18.980 --> 00:42:22.210
there, the rest of the system has to figure
out how to engage with you.

00:42:22.210 --> 00:42:25.520
And they can make a decision to squash you
or try to corrupt you or anything else, but

00:42:25.520 --> 00:42:28.270
more often than not they realize they're in
a process where they've got to think about

00:42:28.270 --> 00:42:29.270
things.

00:42:29.270 --> 00:42:32.520
And so if you look, for example, at the role
of Uber and Careem, which is the Uber competitor-a

00:42:32.520 --> 00:42:36.910
spectacular company in the region-like, when
people come out in the streets and protest

00:42:36.910 --> 00:42:41.310
the defense of Uber over the lousy taxi service,
governments have to take notice.

00:42:41.310 --> 00:42:44.950
And Uber and Careem could only grow to the
way that they've grown because of their ability

00:42:44.950 --> 00:42:47.250
to effectively end-run it, you know, very
quickly.

00:42:47.250 --> 00:42:52.290
A second example of that is when I-when I
was covering, as it were, the Arab world there

00:42:52.290 --> 00:42:57.090
was something like 7.5 million people on LinkedIn
in the Arab world, and LinkedIn had no presence

00:42:57.090 --> 00:42:58.480
in the Arab world.

00:42:58.480 --> 00:43:01.820
LinkedIn is the ultimate go-around of wasta
because it's not who do you know on-it is

00:43:01.820 --> 00:43:05.340
a little bit to the degree that you care about
whose friend is whose friend, but it's your

00:43:05.340 --> 00:43:06.340
track record.

00:43:06.340 --> 00:43:08.180
It is who you are in that kind of a thing.

00:43:08.180 --> 00:43:11.850
So at one level you see what you talked about,
which I think is exactly right, which is this

00:43:11.850 --> 00:43:13.810
unleashing of the bottom-up.

00:43:13.810 --> 00:43:15.550
The top-down matters.

00:43:15.550 --> 00:43:18.520
What's going on obviously in places like Syria
and Iraq are very different than what's going

00:43:18.520 --> 00:43:22.180
on in Dubai-and which, by the way, as a total
aside and for a longer conversation, may very

00:43:22.180 --> 00:43:24.010
well be happening in Saudi Arabia now.

00:43:24.010 --> 00:43:28.360
There are some very interesting developments
there that could be fascinating to watch.

00:43:28.360 --> 00:43:30.880
If it comes to fruition, we don't know.

00:43:30.880 --> 00:43:34.490
But what has happened is governments-like
UAE is a perfect example of this.

00:43:34.490 --> 00:43:36.290
Certainly Singapore is another example of
this.

00:43:36.290 --> 00:43:37.450
They get the joke.

00:43:37.450 --> 00:43:38.470
They look at their countries.

00:43:38.470 --> 00:43:39.470
They look at the future.

00:43:39.470 --> 00:43:42.860
They think about the ramification and they
understand that fundamentally-perhaps not

00:43:42.860 --> 00:43:47.160
perfectly by our standards, though our rule
of law standards are certainly mixed on this-if

00:43:47.160 --> 00:43:53.170
you're on the side of the free movement of
people, ideas, goods and services, and capital,

00:43:53.170 --> 00:43:54.750
this will have a multiplier effect.

00:43:54.750 --> 00:43:59.530
So, as an investor, you need to be looking
at what are those hubs and centers that do

00:43:59.530 --> 00:44:03.320
stuff, still knowing that even the more complicated
places are better than the ones that we love.

00:44:03.320 --> 00:44:08.640
I have to tell you, Egypt's got lots of challenges
and their law system is hardly, you know,

00:44:08.640 --> 00:44:12.050
obvious to me, but if you told me I thought
I'd have a better shot of getting restitution

00:44:12.050 --> 00:44:15.530
in a Cairo court versus a court in China,
I don't know.

00:44:15.530 --> 00:44:19.150
I mean, these are all-these are not for the
soft of heart.

00:44:19.150 --> 00:44:23.280
You go into these things with a risk tolerance
that has to be able to navigate these things,

00:44:23.280 --> 00:44:24.470
keeping that in mind.

00:44:24.470 --> 00:44:29.090
I think one of the biggest opportunities for
someone of your size-not that you would necessarily

00:44:29.090 --> 00:44:35.190
do it, but it would be helpful to the flywheel
of the ecosystem-is growth capital across

00:44:35.190 --> 00:44:36.190
growth markets.

00:44:36.190 --> 00:44:38.080
There is more angel investing than ever before.

00:44:38.080 --> 00:44:39.770
There is a fair amount of A money that's-before.

00:44:39.770 --> 00:44:44.950
I could name you now almost six or seven A-round
funds in the Arab world alone that are 50

00:44:44.950 --> 00:44:45.950
(million dollars) to 100 million (dollars).

00:44:45.950 --> 00:44:49.980
You know, Fadi runs an outstanding fund, which
is, you know, doing very, very successfully

00:44:49.980 --> 00:44:50.980
in that.

00:44:50.980 --> 00:44:54.480
But what often happens is these young people
will build a business to a certain point,

00:44:54.480 --> 00:44:58.430
but there is no money between them and you
to be able to get them to a point where you

00:44:58.430 --> 00:44:59.950
would become interesting.

00:44:59.950 --> 00:45:01.360
And I'm now pushing on this theme.

00:45:01.360 --> 00:45:05.080
And I myself may even participate in this,
but I think that will actually be almost as

00:45:05.080 --> 00:45:11.520
big as a criteria for you, because at the
end of the day, countries will either come

00:45:11.520 --> 00:45:13.050
along or they won't.

00:45:13.050 --> 00:45:16.230
And personally, I believe the ones that won't,
will fall behind where technology is moving

00:45:16.230 --> 00:45:18.260
so quickly they may not be able to catch up.

00:45:18.260 --> 00:45:21.920
But your ability to figure out who those are
in some of these markets is actually becoming

00:45:21.920 --> 00:45:23.390
only more clear, not less so.

00:45:23.390 --> 00:45:27.720
We can always be surprised, but it's becoming
more clear than less so.

00:45:27.720 --> 00:45:32.650
BAYRASLI: I look at the case of Mexico.

00:45:32.650 --> 00:45:38.380
And actually, I talk about how Mexico probably
is the winner, in my book, of the seven countries,

00:45:38.380 --> 00:45:45.410
and I think precisely because you do have
this groundswell of individuals starting businesses,

00:45:45.410 --> 00:45:52.160
even the areas-what was interesting about
being in Mexico-I was there in 2013, and in

00:45:52.160 --> 00:45:55.520
2013 there were still very few e-commerce
companies.

00:45:55.520 --> 00:45:57.580
I was seeing a lot of energy companies.

00:45:57.580 --> 00:46:01.460
I was certainly seeing a lot of B2B companies.

00:46:01.460 --> 00:46:04.810
And when I asked around, particularly the
investors, I said, where are the e-commerce

00:46:04.810 --> 00:46:05.810
companies?

00:46:05.810 --> 00:46:07.660
They all said, why would we-why would we try
to compete with the Americans?

00:46:07.660 --> 00:46:09.810
Like, we're never going to beat Amazon.

00:46:09.810 --> 00:46:15.200
We have to fill the gaps and touch on the
areas where the Americans are weak.

00:46:15.200 --> 00:46:22.930
And, you know, here is where the-you know,
the competitive advantage for Mexico is.

00:46:22.930 --> 00:46:26.430
What I thought was so interesting in Mexico,
and why I think it comes out as a winner,

00:46:26.430 --> 00:46:30.330
is because you have these individuals who
are rushing into these areas where they see

00:46:30.330 --> 00:46:35.580
an opportunity and the government is responding,
and so the Mexican government has done a really

00:46:35.580 --> 00:46:41.550
terrific job in reforming their macroeconomic
policies.

00:46:41.550 --> 00:46:47.860
They have developed funds very much like replicating
what the Israelis did with the Yozma fund,

00:46:47.860 --> 00:46:54.290
and matching-making sure that they guarantee
Mexican investors, that those funds are guaranteed

00:46:54.290 --> 00:47:00.140
and they're encouraging Mexicans to invest
in Mexican entrepreneurs.

00:47:00.140 --> 00:47:07.390
And what the result has been is it's actually
transforming the economic landscape in Mexico.

00:47:07.390 --> 00:47:11.930
It was two years ago where the government
broke up the energy monopolies and they also

00:47:11.930 --> 00:47:13.040
broke up the telecom monopolies.

00:47:13.040 --> 00:47:16.600
I mean, they broke up what Carlos Slim had.

00:47:16.600 --> 00:47:22.920
And for the most part that has happened, you
know, without much violence, because I think

00:47:22.920 --> 00:47:26.620
that those individuals, particularly Carlos
Slim, see what is coming.

00:47:26.620 --> 00:47:30.040
They see that entrepreneurship is not something
that's just happening in their country but

00:47:30.040 --> 00:47:31.820
they see it's happening around the world.

00:47:31.820 --> 00:47:38.140
And if they want to continue to compete and
be as competitive, they actually have to support

00:47:38.140 --> 00:47:39.810
one another as an ecosystem.

00:47:39.810 --> 00:47:46.170
And so I actually was starting to see that
all the components of whether it's the government

00:47:46.170 --> 00:47:50.330
policies, the entrepreneurs, the educational
institutions with the investors all really

00:47:50.330 --> 00:47:55.040
coming together and synchronizing and working
together because they understand that it's

00:47:55.040 --> 00:47:57.930
a game that they all have to be in to win.

00:47:57.930 --> 00:47:59.450
BOAS: Reka (sp)?

00:47:59.450 --> 00:48:03.010
Q: So thank you, both of you.

00:48:03.010 --> 00:48:05.550
BOAS: Put your mic on.

00:48:05.550 --> 00:48:07.840
Q: (Off mic.)

00:48:07.840 --> 00:48:09.360
BOAS: Mic.

00:48:09.360 --> 00:48:12.410
Q: (Comes on mic.)

00:48:12.410 --> 00:48:16.180
So the question I have for you is-
SCHROEDER: Could you repeat that part about-

00:48:16.180 --> 00:48:17.180
(Cross talk, laughter.)

00:48:17.180 --> 00:48:18.180
Q: I was very excited about it.

00:48:18.180 --> 00:48:21.790
So I am curious about impact funding in the
U.S.

00:48:21.790 --> 00:48:26.900
There's this huge desire and market-you know,
Bain recently announced several months ago

00:48:26.900 --> 00:48:31.869
they're creating an impact fund, so there's
potentially this massive marketplace that-and

00:48:31.869 --> 00:48:32.869
a desire.

00:48:32.869 --> 00:48:37.880
And what I have seen from the corporate responsibility
side is this lack of origination.

00:48:37.880 --> 00:48:39.540
And so there's this big gap.

00:48:39.540 --> 00:48:40.540
Obviously there's a lot going on.

00:48:40.540 --> 00:48:41.540
You have to hit the ground.

00:48:41.540 --> 00:48:43.640
You have to be on the ground to understand
what's going on.

00:48:43.640 --> 00:48:48.830
But I think the thing that's most surprising
to a lot of the people on the CSR side, who

00:48:48.830 --> 00:48:53.240
kind of think about this as something they
want to get into, there may be perhaps a desire

00:48:53.240 --> 00:48:58.700
to even accept a, you know, less than 2-in-20
term sheet, which seems to still be the common

00:48:58.700 --> 00:49:04.350
term sheet when you're talking about impact
investing, surprisingly.

00:49:04.350 --> 00:49:08.660
What needs to happen, do you think, to fill
that gap in terms of origination to get enough

00:49:08.660 --> 00:49:12.730
deals in the pipeline that you can really
start to see some of these organizations go

00:49:12.730 --> 00:49:13.730
to scale?

00:49:13.730 --> 00:49:14.730
BAYRASLI: Oh, you're the money guy.

00:49:14.730 --> 00:49:16.200
I'm going to let you answer that.

00:49:16.200 --> 00:49:17.200
(Laughter.)

00:49:17.200 --> 00:49:18.200
SCHROEDER: I'm not a money guy.

00:49:18.200 --> 00:49:23.260
I think there are plenty of companies and
there are plenty of opportunities.

00:49:23.260 --> 00:49:27.390
And it really depends on where you're focused
and what you want to do and, you know, what

00:49:27.390 --> 00:49:29.040
strings attached you're wanting to do.

00:49:29.040 --> 00:49:34.690
I mean, to me the idea that this is a denominator
issue is not the issue.

00:49:34.690 --> 00:49:39.700
Finding the good ones that are fundable becomes
a challenge all of us who do investment in

00:49:39.700 --> 00:49:40.700
this room have to wrestle overall.

00:49:40.700 --> 00:49:42.970
I don't think your challenge is unique in
that.

00:49:42.970 --> 00:49:49.180
But I think very often what happens is that-but
I think CSR is important, but a lot of times

00:49:49.180 --> 00:49:54.220
I think people, they hide behind it as almost
a PR kind of a thing, and so they're not necessarily

00:49:54.220 --> 00:49:56.890
looking for the right criteria in terms of
what they're doing.

00:49:56.890 --> 00:49:59.570
They're trying to check a box of what they're
looking to do.

00:49:59.570 --> 00:50:02.480
And I think at the end of the day, the criteria
of what is a great enterprise and what is

00:50:02.480 --> 00:50:06.140
the impact that it can make, and how big can
it be, and does it justify the terms is the

00:50:06.140 --> 00:50:07.520
focus of it all.

00:50:07.520 --> 00:50:10.360
And they're out there, so I don't think they're
not out there.

00:50:10.360 --> 00:50:15.080
Q: Well, and just as a follow up, I think,
you know, one of the challenges-you know,

00:50:15.080 --> 00:50:19.060
so let's just say I'm working for one of the
good guys who really wants to find that good

00:50:19.060 --> 00:50:25.210
social impact and is willing to take, you
know, a lower profit margin or a longer timeframe

00:50:25.210 --> 00:50:29.830
to receive any profit or return for their
foundation.

00:50:29.830 --> 00:50:34.430
You know, I think the challenge is that we're
seeing a lot of organizations out there who

00:50:34.430 --> 00:50:40.760
maybe-you know, those in the room, you know,
not-those not included, but that we're seeing

00:50:40.760 --> 00:50:43.800
a lot of companies who are, you know, capital
investors or VCs who kind of think, OK, well,

00:50:43.800 --> 00:50:47.310
we're going to do a social impact when it's
another product, it's another opportunity,

00:50:47.310 --> 00:50:49.770
but the term sheets aren't all that different
and they're having trouble getting the money

00:50:49.770 --> 00:50:50.770
out the door.

00:50:50.770 --> 00:50:53.150
SCHROEDER: So look, this is a personal bias-
Q: Yeah.

00:50:53.150 --> 00:50:55.560
SCHROEDER: -and so discount it substantially,
as you should discount anything that comes

00:50:55.560 --> 00:50:56.570
out of my mouth.

00:50:56.570 --> 00:51:02.140
But, you know, "social impact" is a term that
as a-I don't quite understand what it means

00:51:02.140 --> 00:51:04.090
or what it is as a class, right-
BAYRASLI: Amen.

00:51:04.090 --> 00:51:08.740
SCHROEDER: -because I really think, at the
end of the day, that Facebook, which has obviously

00:51:08.740 --> 00:51:12.430
done quite well for its investors, has had
more impact on humanity than almost anything

00:51:12.430 --> 00:51:16.880
I've seen in my life, certainly any of the
top-down aid programs that I witness in Washington.

00:51:16.880 --> 00:51:20.830
I mean, it's the ability to all of a sudden
have people who did not have access to each

00:51:20.830 --> 00:51:22.790
other to have access to each other.

00:51:22.790 --> 00:51:24.990
It has been deeply profound.

00:51:24.990 --> 00:51:29.420
By the way, as an aside, the proceeds of my
book I'm giving away to something called Ruwwad,

00:51:29.420 --> 00:51:33.850
which is a social enterprise that was started
by Fadi Ghandour in Jordan and now it's in

00:51:33.850 --> 00:51:36.560
Budrus and Tripoli, Lebanon, and it's in multiple
places.

00:51:36.560 --> 00:51:41.500
These are community centers built of and for
the community and doing very, very powerful

00:51:41.500 --> 00:51:45.990
things, effectively teaching folks civil dialogue,
civil society, and allowing them to think

00:51:45.990 --> 00:51:49.810
about opportunities where they could get jobs
or work for companies or think about entrepreneurship.

00:51:49.810 --> 00:51:53.730
They also give scholarships so that these
young people get to go out-OK.

00:51:53.730 --> 00:52:00.660
So one of the very, very poor areas of Amman,
Jordan, one of the refugee camps there, you

00:52:00.660 --> 00:52:05.590
know, you go around and you start to interview
the young people in the program and every

00:52:05.590 --> 00:52:10.460
one of them has a smart device that their
family pooled enough money to be able to get

00:52:10.460 --> 00:52:11.510
it for them.

00:52:11.510 --> 00:52:15.390
And every one of them has-says, when you say,
what are you doing with this, they say, well,

00:52:15.390 --> 00:52:16.390
we're using "Face."

00:52:16.390 --> 00:52:17.390
What do you mean?

00:52:17.390 --> 00:52:18.420
We're using Facebook.

00:52:18.420 --> 00:52:19.420
We're using "What."

00:52:19.420 --> 00:52:20.420
That's WhatsApp.

00:52:20.420 --> 00:52:21.420
What are you doing with it?

00:52:21.420 --> 00:52:24.610
And it becomes clear they do all the stuff
we all do with social networks, but all of

00:52:24.610 --> 00:52:29.860
a sudden a life and an existence that was
fundamentally one square mile-which, by the

00:52:29.860 --> 00:52:33.640
way, as you know, any of you who have worked
with city communities here, often people just-their

00:52:33.640 --> 00:52:36.250
entire world outlook is one mile.

00:52:36.250 --> 00:52:38.380
All of a sudden their outlook becomes the
world.

00:52:38.380 --> 00:52:40.660
I mean, that's huge.

00:52:40.660 --> 00:52:45.570
And so I just think that we-I don't use social-I
mean, there are things that-I hate this term,

00:52:45.570 --> 00:52:49.300
so forgive me for using it, but it's the least
descriptive-there are activities happening

00:52:49.300 --> 00:52:53.450
at the bottom of the pyramid that will not
yield 30 percent returns in that classic sense.

00:52:53.450 --> 00:52:56.770
I get that, but I still think they can yield
returns and I think some of them at scale

00:52:56.770 --> 00:52:58.920
will yield many big returns.

00:52:58.920 --> 00:52:59.920
So I understand that.

00:52:59.920 --> 00:53:05.400
There are complicating matters that don't
make the classic for-profit model yield with

00:53:05.400 --> 00:53:06.980
the same ease.

00:53:06.980 --> 00:53:09.750
But I've got to tell you, I'm just uncomfortable
with the term.

00:53:09.750 --> 00:53:13.200
I think that anytime there's a young person
who's ambitious and excited about what they're

00:53:13.200 --> 00:53:16.930
doing and they see it having a multiplier
impact in their community, and then in their

00:53:16.930 --> 00:53:20.040
city, and then in their country, then in their
region, and a lot of these young people are

00:53:20.040 --> 00:53:24.860
even thinking globally, we should be bringing
to the fore the same mindset with them and

00:53:24.860 --> 00:53:29.742
not necessarily put them in a different category.

00:53:29.742 --> 00:53:31.550
BOAS: We have just a couple minutes left but
we have three people who have been waiting

00:53:31.550 --> 00:53:33.460
very patiently.

00:53:33.460 --> 00:53:36.710
So if you wouldn't mind, maybe we'll do Leah
and then Zeeshan.

00:53:36.710 --> 00:53:39.700
And then, Julie (sp), do you still have your
question?

00:53:39.700 --> 00:53:42.570
OK, then we have only two very patient people.

00:53:42.570 --> 00:53:47.150
So maybe let's hear both questions and then
you can maybe answer them together.

00:53:47.150 --> 00:53:50.000
Q: OK, Leah Pedersen Thomas, VitalPet.

00:53:50.000 --> 00:53:54.000
Unintentionally so, my question is a bit of
a tack-on to what Reka (ph) said, so it will

00:53:54.000 --> 00:53:55.720
probably be shortened by virtue of that.

00:53:55.720 --> 00:54:01.740
But essentially you had mentioned, Elmira,
that the West isn't really taking seriously

00:54:01.740 --> 00:54:04.869
or getting this concept of these entrepreneurs.

00:54:04.869 --> 00:54:09.720
And then obviously, Chris, you raised the
example-the great example of Nigeria and technology

00:54:09.720 --> 00:54:13.130
platform to help vendors improve procurement.

00:54:13.130 --> 00:54:21.700
And so with that, is the evolving narrative
of social impact investing, social entrepreneurship,

00:54:21.700 --> 00:54:27.870
as well as labeling these genuine entrepreneurs
who are trying to create jobs as well as fix

00:54:27.870 --> 00:54:33.540
systemic problems and have an ROI-is it the
labeling of them that's actually diminishing

00:54:33.540 --> 00:54:36.040
this attention?

00:54:36.040 --> 00:54:40.980
And are we doing harm to them more so by labeling
or couching them as social impact and social

00:54:40.980 --> 00:54:41.980
entrepreneurs?

00:54:41.980 --> 00:54:45.220
BAYRASLI: I mean, I don't think that we're
doing any damage.

00:54:45.220 --> 00:54:49.590
I mean, those labels, I don't see them in
the places I travel to.

00:54:49.590 --> 00:54:56.470
I mean, I think where I hear the word "social
entrepreneur" or "social impact fund," I hear

00:54:56.470 --> 00:54:58.430
here in the United States.

00:54:58.430 --> 00:55:03.000
I virtually don't-I don't hear it in any of
the countries I've traveled to.

00:55:03.000 --> 00:55:08.680
And if I have heard it, it's because there's
a Western NGO or someone from the West who

00:55:08.680 --> 00:55:14.790
has that intention of coming in and trying
to create either a social enterprise and support

00:55:14.790 --> 00:55:15.790
it in that way.

00:55:15.790 --> 00:55:16.790
In terms-yes?

00:55:16.790 --> 00:55:18.970
Q: Oh, no, I'm sorry to interrupt.

00:55:18.970 --> 00:55:23.760
Just for a clarification, I meant it within
the context of the Western world and our putting

00:55:23.760 --> 00:55:28.140
the label, recognizing that the label probably
doesn't exist locally, but is our label-enforcement

00:55:28.140 --> 00:55:33.350
of that label, is it somehow harming but-
SCHROEDER: Sounds like you agree.

00:55:33.350 --> 00:55:34.350
BAYRASLI: Yeah.

00:55:34.350 --> 00:55:38.240
I mean, I do-I do actually think, because
I think we're still looking-you know, I started

00:55:38.240 --> 00:55:42.240
out and I started saying how, you know, when
we say the word "entrepreneur" in the context

00:55:42.240 --> 00:55:46.010
of Africa, Asia, Latin America, and the Middle
East, we think about it as, you know, like

00:55:46.010 --> 00:55:47.010
something-microfinance.

00:55:47.010 --> 00:55:51.540
We think about, you know, handicrafts and
we're thinking about something micro.

00:55:51.540 --> 00:55:57.220
But the reality is, inasmuch as there are
those enterprises going on around the world,

00:55:57.220 --> 00:56:04.240
there are numerous and, I think, far greater
number of enterprises that are really scalable

00:56:04.240 --> 00:56:07.900
and that can have the return on it.

00:56:07.900 --> 00:56:12.070
And so I think, you know, again, it's about
the mindset that we're having and how we're

00:56:12.070 --> 00:56:15.090
looking at entrepreneurship outside the world.

00:56:15.090 --> 00:56:20.180
And I think what Chris and I-I think what
we try to do with our books is really try

00:56:20.180 --> 00:56:26.180
to change that mindset and the thinking about
how we're approaching and seeing the greater

00:56:26.180 --> 00:56:27.180
world.

00:56:27.180 --> 00:56:31.830
And we need to do it because it's happening
and they're not waiting for us, and they're

00:56:31.830 --> 00:56:35.730
creating the enterprises, and the train has
left the station.

00:56:35.730 --> 00:56:40.420
And we're still talking about, you know, development
and we're talking about social impact, and

00:56:40.420 --> 00:56:42.070
the world is a different place.

00:56:42.070 --> 00:56:45.920
You know, the United States is not the savior
anymore.

00:56:45.920 --> 00:56:49.070
Countries are out there and they're solving
their own problems and they're doing it with

00:56:49.070 --> 00:56:51.650
or without our capital and our assistance.

00:56:51.650 --> 00:56:55.820
SCHROEDER: I must say, the region that I've
spent a lot of time with lately, I don't think

00:56:55.820 --> 00:56:59.790
they think our savior batting average is that
great to begin with.

00:56:59.790 --> 00:57:00.790
(Laughter.)

00:57:00.790 --> 00:57:01.970
BOAS: Zeeshan?

00:57:01.970 --> 00:57:08.130
Q: Zeeshan Zaidi with Live Nation Entertainment,
although previously I was at several startups

00:57:08.130 --> 00:57:10.740
in the New York area.

00:57:10.740 --> 00:57:17.130
How do you think about or quantify the impact
that this entrepreneurial revolution in these

00:57:17.130 --> 00:57:23.460
emerging economies or developing economies
is having on economic development, right,

00:57:23.460 --> 00:57:28.400
or traditional metrics that you think about
when you think about economic development,

00:57:28.400 --> 00:57:31.640
like per capita income or employment?

00:57:31.640 --> 00:57:37.830
Is it just that some jobs are created and
some fortunes are made?

00:57:37.830 --> 00:57:41.940
Or is it something broader like, you know,
the quality of life of a lot of people is

00:57:41.940 --> 00:57:43.800
improved or problems are solved?

00:57:43.800 --> 00:57:47.810
But is it-you know, is it moving the needle
in terms of the metrics that, for example,

00:57:47.810 --> 00:57:49.580
the World Bank would look at?

00:57:49.580 --> 00:57:52.390
Or are those metrics just not as relevant
anymore?

00:57:52.390 --> 00:57:54.780
SCHROEDER: Some metrics are not relevant and
never were.

00:57:54.780 --> 00:57:58.770
Some metrics are certainly provocative and
all conversations of data should be addressable

00:57:58.770 --> 00:57:59.770
overall.

00:57:59.770 --> 00:58:05.070
We are in a revolution in process, and one
of the things-because I did-other people did

00:58:05.070 --> 00:58:09.400
most of the work but I did a paper-I vowed
I'd never do anything with a think tank but

00:58:09.400 --> 00:58:13.350
the Atlantic Council put together an amazing
bipartisan group with Madeleine Albright and

00:58:13.350 --> 00:58:16.850
Steve Hadley and then really wanted to look
at the lens of the Middle East differently.

00:58:16.850 --> 00:58:20.310
And so we pulled together a bunch of these
rock stars from the Middle East and put together

00:58:20.310 --> 00:58:24.570
a thought paper, you know, about this kind
of a question and about thinking about the

00:58:24.570 --> 00:58:25.570
metrics.

00:58:25.570 --> 00:58:28.850
And one of the points that we made-and I believe
very well, because in the early days of it

00:58:28.850 --> 00:58:32.330
I really got slammed by macro economists who
were effectively saying this stuff doesn't

00:58:32.330 --> 00:58:33.330
matter.

00:58:33.330 --> 00:58:35.254
And I'm like, let me be very clear to you-because
I've actually researched this; I'm not making

00:58:35.254 --> 00:58:39.380
this up off the top of my head-if you see
what macroeconomists said about the automobile

00:58:39.380 --> 00:58:43.740
in 1910 and its ramifications on the economy;
if you asked them about what television meant

00:58:43.740 --> 00:58:48.980
to 1938, macro to the economy; if you asked
them to talk about air conditioning in 1950,

00:58:48.980 --> 00:58:53.260
the Internet in 1992, mobile in 2001, and
think about what they're saying today about

00:58:53.260 --> 00:58:57.510
robotics and AI and genomics and that kind
of stuff-we're dealing with something that

00:58:57.510 --> 00:58:59.550
is in process.

00:58:59.550 --> 00:59:03.619
Some of it is anecdotal, some of it is better
than anecdotal but these are still relatively

00:59:03.619 --> 00:59:04.700
early days.

00:59:04.700 --> 00:59:08.990
The question is, what percentage of the economy
do we think is going to be focused in innovation

00:59:08.990 --> 00:59:12.921
or technology versus where it is today, because
I'll make the step further-and I think this

00:59:12.921 --> 00:59:13.921
is important.

00:59:13.921 --> 00:59:15.660
I wish I had underscored it before.

00:59:15.660 --> 00:59:19.140
I think the startups and the entrepreneurship
and the breadth that is being unleashed around

00:59:19.140 --> 00:59:21.220
the world because of technology is profound.

00:59:21.220 --> 00:59:25.520
And we know that most jobs are created by
the smaller end of the sector to begin with

00:59:25.520 --> 00:59:26.520
anyways.

00:59:26.520 --> 00:59:31.190
But every company on earth now is a technology-is
a software company in some form.

00:59:31.190 --> 00:59:32.190
Every one.

00:59:32.190 --> 00:59:34.600
You know, go to Starbucks and order a cup
of coffee.

00:59:34.600 --> 00:59:36.570
Go look under the hood of your car.

00:59:36.570 --> 00:59:38.660
Everything now is a technology company, and
even more so.

00:59:38.660 --> 00:59:41.430
And the ones that don't understand that are
going to fall back.

00:59:41.430 --> 00:59:45.501
So all the factors that she's described so
beautifully around the world and some of the

00:59:45.501 --> 00:59:49.340
stuff that I've talked about there is actually
the similar factors that will have all aspects

00:59:49.340 --> 00:59:51.040
of the economy overall.

00:59:51.040 --> 00:59:54.740
And so I don't think it's a matter of whether;
it's a matter of when, and we have to engage

00:59:54.740 --> 00:59:56.440
it in those terms.

00:59:56.440 --> 01:00:02.680
BAYRASLI: I mean, what I've seen is I think
they are not only creating the jobs but, you

01:00:02.680 --> 01:00:07.350
know, going back to how I define entrepreneurship,
I think they're remaking the socioeconomic

01:00:07.350 --> 01:00:08.350
landscape.

01:00:08.350 --> 01:00:12.630
And I've seen that wherever I have traveled,
and the governments realize that.

01:00:12.630 --> 01:00:18.520
And the governments are responding by creating
the funds and changing their policies and

01:00:18.520 --> 01:00:21.230
really focusing in on the entrepreneurship.

01:00:21.230 --> 01:00:30.080
China, the government of China-I mean, entrepreneurship
is one of the top priorities for the Communist

01:00:30.080 --> 01:00:37.390
Party, ironically, because inasmuch as China's
fortunes have, you know, gone in a really

01:00:37.390 --> 01:00:42.570
positive direction and, you know, it's the
second-largest economy in the world, you still

01:00:42.570 --> 01:00:47.810
have 500 million people in that country who
live below the poverty line.

01:00:47.810 --> 01:00:51.430
And the Chinese government needs to figure
out, how do we actually bring those people

01:00:51.430 --> 01:00:52.500
into the fold?

01:00:52.500 --> 01:00:58.070
How do we actually get them to be on par with
the rest of the country?

01:00:58.070 --> 01:00:59.570
How do we create those jobs?

01:00:59.570 --> 01:01:02.750
And they certainly believe it's through-it's
through entrepreneurs.

01:01:02.750 --> 01:01:06.290
SCHROEDER: Most of these countries, particularly
in the Middle East but across the world now,

01:01:06.290 --> 01:01:09.270
have 30 (percent), 40 (percent), 50 percent
of their population is under 35.

01:01:09.270 --> 01:01:12.240
Thirty percent unemployment there, we have
a big problem; 50 percent unemployment in

01:01:12.240 --> 01:01:13.950
some cases.

01:01:13.950 --> 01:01:15.820
So the governments that are smart are getting
it.

01:01:15.820 --> 01:01:20.030
When the government of UAE says three months
ago, we will celebrate the last barrel of

01:01:20.030 --> 01:01:24.020
oil, that's a hell of a-and focus on innovation
and technology and entrepreneurship, that's

01:01:24.020 --> 01:01:25.390
a hell of a statement.

01:01:25.390 --> 01:01:26.510
And China is a good example.

01:01:26.510 --> 01:01:28.220
India is another good example.

01:01:28.220 --> 01:01:31.450
So I think that the ones who get it will have
great ramifications.

01:01:31.450 --> 01:01:34.460
The ones who doubt it may truly fall behind
for good.

01:01:34.460 --> 01:01:38.760
BOAS: Well, look, we could do this for another
several hours-

01:01:38.760 --> 01:01:39.760
SCHROEDER: Thank you.

01:01:39.760 --> 01:01:40.760
BOAS: -but I want to be respectful of everybody's
time.

01:01:40.760 --> 01:01:52.050
Thank you so much to you both for doing this,
and thank you all.

01:01:52.050 --> 01:01:54.770
BAYRASLI: Thank you.

01:01:54.770 --> 01:01:55.770
(Applause.)

01:01:55.770 --> 01:01:56.579
(END)

